# How this course was made (W3C PROV-O): written from the sources below,
# then checked in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://github.com/standard-for-public-code/standard-for-public-code/tree/0.8.1> ,
              <https://www.standardforpubliccode.org/> ,
              <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ,
                       <#review-6> ,
                       <#review-7> ,
                       <#review-8> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the course was planned by AI agents from research notes on every criterion of release 0.8.1 of the Standard for Public Code, read from the release's own text, on its framing pages (definitions, foreword, readers' guide, how a criterion is laid out and assessed) and on its outside context; three outlines were drafted from different angles (the Standard's own order, a codebase's life, and the roles the Standard addresses) and merged into one, and the chapters were then written from it in English. The working notes and the outline are kept with the course's sources, not in the library."@en ,
                 "The course was checked by machine (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks, and a second SHACL validator) and by AI agents against the text of release 0.8.1; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Selection: what a learner needs to read and apply the Standard: what public code is, who the Standard is for and what it leaves out, how a criterion is laid out, what its MUST, SHOULD and OPTIONAL keywords mean and how it is assessed, then every requirement of all 16 criteria, in the Standard's own order. The people, teams and codebases in the questions' scenarios are invented. Left out on purpose: the Standard's own governance, steering and release history, its assessment of itself, the changes made after release 0.8.1, the exact counts of its keywords, any reading of the order of its criteria as a ranking, and points the release text leaves uncertain."@en ,
                 "Structure: 9 chapters of 30 steps in all. Each step is a short theory text, read in two chunks, and two or three multiple-choice questions on it; each chapter has review questions that combine its steps and are asked only in its final review (four in the first chapter, which no later chapter practises, and two in every other): 62 step questions and 20 review questions, 82 questions in all. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors) of similar length and form, each with a note on why it is wrong. The course is in English; its title, description and keywords are also in Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0, like the Standard itself and most of the library. Its theory, questions, options and notes are in the course's own words, with short requirement texts quoted from the Standard, which is CC0 1.0 and so may be quoted without conditions. RFC 2119 and the Standard's website were used for facts only, with a few short phrases from RFC 2119 quoted and attributed."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the whole release, with the library checker (npm run library:check), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 6, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, checks the course's outline by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release, and with a second, independent SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck) over the same shapes."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The release conforms to every shape, profile and rule the checker applies to it, and the crosscheck agrees."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Outline: facts and question design (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: each chapter of the merged outline checked by two AI agents: one that tried to refute every theory point, answer and wrong option against the text of release 0.8.1, line by line, and one for the questions' design; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 75 findings across the nine chapters, all fixed in the outline before the chapters were written and none kept as they were; among them claims that went further than the release (such as that it names no language or platform), questions with two defensible answers, a requirement level misstated, fronts that gave the answer away, off-topic wrong options, and backs that stood out by their form."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Outline as a whole (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the nine chapters of the outline read together by an AI agent against the release text, for criteria or requirements left out, contradictions between chapters, terms used differently, facts taught twice, theory that relies on a later chapter, the balance of chapter sizes and question forms, and correct options that stand out by their length; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: one missing requirement was added, one contradiction between chapters was resolved, three facts taught twice became back references, one forward reference was removed, two names used for different people were changed, a second review question was added to each chapter after the first, and backs and wrong options were reworded so that the correct option is seldom the longest (15 of 82, down from 32 of 74); a few points were kept on purpose, with a reason, such as real criterion names as wrong options."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter text: facts, format and language (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: each chapter as written, reviewed by AI agents for every fact, quotation, answer and wrong option against the release text, for the release's format rules and the questions' design, and for the English; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 78 findings across the nine chapters, all fixed in the text but one, which another finding's fix replaced; among them a requirement level understated, quotations cut short or spliced, wrong options that could be true, notes that overstated a rule, a real city in an invented scenario, and backs that stood out by their length."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Course as a whole (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the nine chapters as written, read together by an AI agent against the release text, for facts taught twice, terms and names used differently, wrong options that could be defended, runs of backs that stand out, and consistent typography; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 10 findings, all fixed: a wrong option that could be defended, two definitions taught again as new (now back references), a run of three backs that were the longest option, wrong options drawn from criteria not yet taught, a role sentence that read wrongly, inconsistent quotation marks and apostrophes, two near-identical names, and a point made twice."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-12T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Title, description and keywords in Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the deck's title, description and keywords, translated by an AI agent from the English and checked by translating every text back into English and comparing it with the English; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: two drifts were fixed (a German word for policy that read back as guidelines, and a Spanish phrase whose reference was unclear); the translations now match the English, and the Standard's name is kept in English as a proper name, with a gloss in Korean."@en .

<#review-6>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: questions that work as flip cards too (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every question of the course read by AI agents as it is later studied flip-and-grade, its front alone with no options, for questions whose answer is one of several valid ones or that make sense only with their options; each finding put to three more AI agents that tried to refute it. The questions upheld were rewritten and each revision checked by three AI agents, for recall from the front alone, for the multiple choice (one correct option; wrong options plausible, of the same form and wrong for the new question) and for its facts against the text of release 0.8.1 as the steps quote it, and rewritten until all three passed; the release checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: of 82 questions, 2 are asked anew, their backs unchanged: one names the two MUSTs it does not ask for, and one asks what open code lets an agency do about its supplier, its three wrong options retired and replaced by three that each share one half of the answer, so that no option gives the answer away by its form."@en .

<#review-8>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-22T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 4: questions that work as flip cards too (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every question of the course read by AI agents as it is later studied flip-and-grade, its front alone with no options, for questions whose answer is one of several valid ones, that make sense only with their options or that do not ask for what the back answers; each finding put to three more AI agents, and kept when two upheld it. The questions upheld were rewritten by an AI agent and each revision checked by three others, for recall from the front alone, for the multiple choice (one correct option; wrong options plausible, of the same form and wrong for the new question) and for its facts against the step's theory, and rewritten until all three passed; the release compared with version 3 and checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: of 82 questions, 2 are asked anew (both with a back reworded to answer the new question, its meaning kept); 4 wrong options that no longer fitted are retired and 4 new ones added, and kept ones are reworded to the new question with the same wrong claim. Every chapter, step and card keeps its id."@en .

<#review-7>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-17T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 3: the Foundation for Public Code's reviews (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: what the course says about the Foundation for Public Code, checked by AI agents against the foundation's own pages and blog, the Standard's release notes, README and governance, and its pull requests and issues after release 0.8.1, each finding checked again by an AI agent that tried to refute it; the reworded step and question checked against the text of release 0.8.1, and the release checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the release's sentence that the review template is \"used by Foundation for Public Code stewards for codebase review\" is a leftover from before 2024, when the foundation wound down its European office, which had looked after the Standard and its codebase reviews; its codebase stewards left in 2024, no review by the foundation against the Standard was found after that, and no formal review offered by the Standard's community was found. One step and one question are reworded so that the template is the tool for recording a verdict on every requirement, with no steward, and the foundation stays only in the sources' attribution."@en .

# The sources (W3C PROV-O): what the course took from each, and on what license.
<https://github.com/standard-for-public-code/standard-for-public-code/tree/0.8.1>
    dcterms:title "Standard for Public Code, release 0.8.1: source repository" ;
    dcterms:creator "Standard for Public Code Authors and the Foundation for Public Code, 2025" ;
    rdfs:comment "The release's README.md: \"The standard is licensed under CC 0, which also applies to all illustrations and the documentation.\"; its LICENSE is CC0 1.0 Universal, and every file carries \"SPDX-License-Identifier: CC0-1.0\". Content taken: the course paraphrases the Standard and quotes short requirement texts verbatim, which CC0 allows without conditions. Reliability: authoritative (the Standard itself, release 0.8.1 of 28 April 2025). Read in October 2026, at tag 0.8.1. Used for: every fact about the Standard: what public code is, who the Standard is for and its scope, how its criteria are laid out, worded and assessed, and the requirements of all 16 criteria."@en .

<https://www.standardforpubliccode.org/>
    dcterms:title "Standard for Public Code" ;
    dcterms:creator "Standard for Public Code Authors and the Foundation for Public Code" ;
    rdfs:comment "The Standard's website, built from the same repository; the release's README.md: \"The standard is licensed under CC 0, which also applies to all illustrations and the documentation.\", and the site's footer, as fetched in October 2026, states CC0. Used for facts only: nothing copied from it beyond what the tagged release holds. Reliability: authoritative (the Standard's official website). Read in October 2026, when it published version 0.8.1. Used for: confirming that 0.8.1, of 28 April 2025, is the version the site publishes, and the address the course points learners to."@en .

<https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119>
    dcterms:title "Key words for use in RFCs to Indicate Requirement Levels (RFC 2119, BCP 14)" ;
    dcterms:creator "S. Bradner, IETF, 1997" ;
    rdfs:comment "The RFC's Status of this Memo: \"Distribution of this memo is unlimited.\" (the IETF Trust's terms for RFC text were not re-verified). Used for facts only: the keywords' meanings are paraphrased, with a few short phrases quoted and attributed, such as \"an absolute requirement of the specification\". Reliability: normative (the IETF Best Current Practice the Standard names for its keywords). Read in October 2026, at https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119.txt. Used for: the meaning of MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, MAY and OPTIONAL in chapter 2, and the MUST, SHOULD and OPTIONAL contrasts in the criteria chapters."@en .

# Chapter 1: What public code is.
<#ch-public-code>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    dcterms:title "What public code is"@en ;
    dcterms:description "What the Standard for Public Code is, what it means by public code, code, codebase and context, why public organizations choose public code, who the Standard speaks to and what it does not promise. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-public-code-r01> ,
                              <#q-public-code-r02> ,
                              <#q-public-code-r03> ,
                              <#q-public-code-r04> .

<#ch-public-code-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-public-code> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## A standard for public code

The Standard for Public Code is “a set of criteria that supports public organizations in developing and maintaining software and policy together”. It guides public organizations “seeking to successfully collaborate on open source solutions with similar organizations in other places”, and it is meant for “all levels of government, from supranational to municipal”.

You need nobody's permission to apply it: “just go ahead, it's an open standard and free for anyone to use”. It is still “in a draft format”, being prepared for a version 1.0 release. This course follows release 0.8.1, published on April 28, 2025.

---

The Standard defines public code as “open source software developed by public organizations, together with the policy and guidance needed for collaboration and reuse”. It is both source code, such as software and algorithms, and public policy, executed by humans or machines.

Public code is a building block that others can re-implement in their local context, take as a starting point to continue development, or use as a basis for learning. It is released into the public domain or under an open license that lets others view it, reuse it and produce derivative works.

The Standard “is meant to be time and technology independent”. Its requirements prescribe no programming language, platform or software stack; tools such as Git or GitHub appear only as examples. (The criteria do require English as one authoritative language, but that is a human language, not a technology.)"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-public-code-1a> ,
                         <#q-public-code-1b> .

<#q-public-code-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A ministry wants its new permit system to count as public code in the Standard's sense. Besides open source software developed by public organizations, what does the definition include?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The policy and guidance needed for collaboration and reuse"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The Standard defines public code as open source software from public organizations together with the policy and guidance that others need to collaborate on it and reuse it. Software alone is not the whole of it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-1a-d3> .

<#q-public-code-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A certificate showing the system meets all sixteen criteria"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The definition says nothing about certificates; meeting the criteria is not part of what public code is."@en .

<#q-public-code-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A hosting contract with an approved public cloud provider"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The definition names no hosting arrangement, and the Standard is meant to be technology independent."@en .

<#q-public-code-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Formal approval from a national open source agency"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No approval body appears in the definition; public code is software plus the policy and guidance for reuse."@en .

<#q-public-code-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A vendor asks whether adopting the Standard for Public Code will push its clients toward one programming language or platform. What does the Standard say about itself?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It is meant to be time and technology independent"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The Standard states that it is meant to be time and technology independent. Its requirements describe practices such as openness, review and documentation; tools such as Git or GitHub appear only as examples."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-1b-d3> .

<#q-public-code-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is meant to settle on one shared software stack"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Standard prescribes no software stack; it says it is meant to be technology independent."@en .

<#q-public-code-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is meant to recommend one stack for each level of government"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Standard names no stack for any level of government; it calls itself technology independent."@en .

<#q-public-code-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It is meant to name its preferred tools in each criterion"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The criteria describe practices, not tools, and Git or GitHub appear only as examples; the Standard calls itself time and technology independent."@en .

<#ch-public-code-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-public-code> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Code, codebases and contexts

The Standard uses everyday words in a precise way. **Code** is “Any explicitly described system of rules.” That includes laws, policy and ordinances as well as the source code used to build software: “Both of these are rules, some executed by humans and others by machines.”

A **codebase** is “Any discrete package of code (both source and policy), the tests and the documentation required to implement a piece of policy or software”. It can be, for example, a document or a version-control repository.

And when the Standard says policy, it means policy “created and adopted by public organizations such as governments and municipalities”.

---

Where code is used matters too. “Two contexts are different if they are different public organizations or different departments for which there is not one decision maker that could make collaboration happen naturally.” Several criteria build on this idea, for example reuse in different contexts and review by someone from a different context.

The general public are the “end users of the code and the services based upon it”, such as the residents of a city.

The Standard leaves two terms to others. For open source it points to the Open Source Initiative's Open Source Definition, and for open standards to the same organization's Open Standard Requirements."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-public-code-2a> ,
                         <#q-public-code-2b> .

<#q-public-code-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Two teams in a city's IT department report to the same director. Each team keeps its own budget and runs its own installation of a shared permit app. In the Standard's terms, are they different contexts?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, because one decision maker could make them collaborate"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Contexts are different when they are different organizations, or departments with no single decision maker who could make collaboration happen naturally. A shared director is exactly such a decision maker."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-2a-d3> .

<#q-public-code-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, because the two teams keep separate budgets"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Budgets are not the test; the test is whether one decision maker could make them collaborate."@en .

<#q-public-code-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, because each team runs its own installation"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Separate installations do not make separate contexts; the Standard's test is about who decides."@en .

<#q-public-code-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, because different contexts must be in different countries"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Two public organizations in the same country can be different contexts; borders play no part in the test."@en .

<#q-public-code-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A municipality's housing rules are written down in an ordinance, and caseworkers apply them by hand. Is that ordinance code in the Standard's sense?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes, code is any explicitly described system of rules"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The Standard counts laws, policy and ordinances as code, just like source code. Some code is executed by humans and some by machines; both are rules."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-2b-d3> .

<#q-public-code-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, code means only source code that builds software"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Standard's definition of code explicitly includes laws, policy and ordinances."@en .

<#q-public-code-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only once the rules are implemented in software"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The ordinance is already code; being executed by humans rather than machines does not change that."@en .

<#q-public-code-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only if the ordinance is published in a machine format"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Machine readability is not part of the Standard's definition of code."@en .

<#ch-public-code-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-public-code> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Why public code

Software has become public infrastructure. The Standard's foreword notes that it is “increasingly not just the expression of existing policy but the originator of new policy, for example where algorithms decide which districts need extra social services or policing”. “Software code has increasingly started to equal legal code”, and so “Software should therefore be subject to the principles of democratic governance.”

The old way of buying software has its costs. Public organizations that bought complete solutions found that they:

- “can't change their software to reflect changing policy”
- “don't have access to their data as it's locked into proprietary systems”
- “are asked to pay ever increasing license fees”

---

Public code works differently. As other administrations use it, they contribute back. Many eyes spotting flaws make it more secure, and many contributors share the maintenance, which adds up to “lower risk public infrastructure”.

There is an economic side as well. “Because the code is open, the public administration can change vendors if they need to.” Open code also lets an administration procure smaller contracts, which local small and medium enterprises can bid on more easily.

Someone still does the work: public code can be developed by “permanent in-house development teams, contractors or outsourced suppliers”. And to use existing public code, you “specify in your budget and project design that your new solution will use that codebase”."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-public-code-3a> ,
                         <#q-public-code-3b> .

<#q-public-code-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A city bought a complete case-management system years ago. A new law changes who qualifies, the vendor quotes a high price for the change, and nobody else may touch the code. Had the system been public code, what does the foreword say the city could do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Change vendors when it needs to, because the code is open"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The foreword names vendor choice as an economic benefit of public code: open code is not tied to one supplier, so the administration can hand the work to another vendor or its own team."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-3a-d3> .

<#q-public-code-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Stop paying for development, because open code is free"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Public code is still developed by in-house teams, contractors or suppliers; the benefit is choice, not free work."@en .

<#q-public-code-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Let residents vote on the change, because the code is public"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Democratic governance is the foreword's argument for opening code, not a residents' vote on each change; what open code gives here is vendor choice."@en .

<#q-public-code-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Skip procurement, because open code needs no contract"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The foreword still describes procuring public code, for example by naming the codebase in the budget and project design."@en .

<#q-public-code-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "The foreword argues that software should be subject to the principles of democratic governance. On what ground?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Software increasingly executes, even originates, public policy"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Software now carries out policy set through democratic procedures and sometimes creates new policy, so the foreword says software code has started to equal legal code."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-3b-d3> .

<#q-public-code-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Open source licenses require democratic oversight of the code"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The argument is about what software does in society, not about a license condition."@en .

<#q-public-code-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Public money may only be spent on software voters approve"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The foreword makes no such claim about voters approving spending."@en .

<#q-public-code-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Software usually outlives the laws it was written for"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The foreword does not argue from software's lifespan but from software executing and creating policy."@en .

<#ch-public-code-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-public-code> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Who it is for, and what it does not do

The Standard is “for the people who create and reuse public code”:

- public policy makers
- business and project managers
- developers and designers

These people work at institutions, organizations and administrations in the public sector, and at “consultancies and vendors of information technology and policy services to public organizations”. So a vendor that builds software for governments is one of its readers too.

Some people are left out on purpose: “It is not aimed at public organizations' end users (residents or citizens), journalists or academics.”

---

The Standard also sets out a limitation of scope. It “is not meant to cover individual implementations of a codebase”, so it “does not tell implementers how to comply with their organization's local technical infrastructure or legal framework”.

It does not compete with other standards either. It “does not aim to replace quality standards, like the ISO 25000 series, or those focusing on security, like the OpenSSF Best Practices Badge, but to synergize well with them”.

And it makes no promise about people. The Standard “will not guarantee that a community will spring into existence”: that “still requires proactiveness and ambition beyond making the codebase collaboration ready”."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-public-code-4a> ,
                         <#q-public-code-4b> .

<#q-public-code-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "An IT vendor that builds software for several provinces wonders whether the Standard for Public Code speaks to it at all. What does the Standard say?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes, vendors serving public organizations are among its readers"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The foreword lists consultancies and vendors of IT and policy services to public organizations, alongside public-sector institutions, as the places its readers work."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-4a-d3> .

<#q-public-code-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, it is written for public-sector staff alone"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The foreword names consultancies and vendors serving public organizations next to public-sector institutions."@en .

<#q-public-code-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only if the vendor also releases its own products as open source"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The foreword names vendors of IT services to public organizations as readers, with no condition about their own products."@en .

<#q-public-code-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only through the public organization that hires it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Vendors are addressed directly as readers, not only through their clients."@en .

<#q-public-code-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A ministry's codebase meets every criterion, and its manager now expects other governments to start contributing. What does the Standard say about that expectation?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Meeting the criteria does not guarantee a community"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The Standard's limitation of scope says it will not guarantee that a community springs into existence; that still takes proactiveness and ambition beyond being ready for collaboration."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-4b-d3> .

<#q-public-code-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A community forms once the code is openly licensed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An open license makes contributing possible; nothing in the Standard promises that anyone will."@en .

<#q-public-code-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Others will contribute once it fits their legal framework"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Fitting local legal frameworks is outside the Standard's scope, and nothing in it promises contributions."@en .

<#q-public-code-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Others will contribute once it also earns an ISO 25000 certificate"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Standard aims to synergize well with ISO 25000 quality standards, not to replace them, and it promises no community either way."@en .

<#q-public-code-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Lena, a policy maker at a provincial government, drafts eligibility rules for a subsidy, and the province's developers turn them into software. A neighboring province with its own decision makers wants to reuse the result. What, in the Standard's terms, is the public code here?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The software and the eligibility rules, packaged for reuse"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Public code is software and policy together with what others need to reuse it, and code includes written rules such as policy. The neighboring province is a different context, since no single decision maker spans both."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r01-d3> .

<#q-public-code-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The software, since the rules count as law rather than code"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Standard counts policy and laws as code, and public code includes policy."@en .

<#q-public-code-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The rules, since the software merely implements them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Public code is both source code and public policy; the software is part of it, not an implementation outside it."@en .

<#q-public-code-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The software and the rules, but only once the neighbor reuses them"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Being public code does not depend on anyone reusing it; the neighbor being a different context matters for reuse, not for what counts as public code."@en .

<#q-public-code-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Omar, a business manager at a regional agency, proposes building its new grants system as public code with a local contractor. His director asks two things: what does the open code let the agency do about its supplier, and what does the Standard say it will not guarantee?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It can change vendors later, but no community is guaranteed"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Because the code is open, the agency can hand the work to another vendor later. But the Standard says it will not guarantee that a community springs into existence: that still takes proactiveness and ambition."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r02-d3> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r02-d4> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r02-d5> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r02-d6> .

<#q-public-code-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It pays nothing for development, and others take over upkeep"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Public code is still developed by in-house teams, contractors or suppliers; shared maintenance is a hope, not a promise."@en .

<#q-public-code-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It can change vendors later, and other agencies must contribute"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The first half is right, but nothing in the Standard obliges anyone to contribute."@en .

<#q-public-code-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It needs no contract, and the Standard covers its local law"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Public code is still procured, and the Standard does not tell implementers how to comply with their legal framework."@en .

<#q-public-code-r02-d4>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It can change vendors later, but no open license is guaranteed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Public code is by definition released into the public domain or under an open license. What the Standard will not guarantee is that a community springs into existence."@en .

<#q-public-code-r02-d5>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It can stop paying for development, but no community is guaranteed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Someone still does the work: in-house teams, contractors or suppliers. What open code lets the agency do is change vendors if it needs to."@en .

<#q-public-code-r02-d6>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It can stop paying for development, but no open license is guaranteed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Someone still does the work, and public code is by definition released into the public domain or under an open license. Open code lets the agency change vendors, and the Standard will not guarantee that a community springs into existence."@en .

<#q-public-code-r03>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Two neighboring municipalities, each with its own council, need a parking-permit system. One of them already runs one as public code. What fits the Standard's reasoning for the other?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Reuse it from a different context and share upkeep with the first"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "With separate councils there is no single decision maker, so they are different contexts. The foreword's case for public code is that other administrations reuse it and contribute back, sharing maintenance."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-r03-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r03-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r03-d3> .

<#q-public-code-r03-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Wait until one decision maker governs both, then share the code"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Collaboration across different contexts, with no shared decision maker, is exactly what public code is meant to enable."@en .

<#q-public-code-r03-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Buy its own complete solution to keep the two contexts apart"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The foreword warns that complete solutions can lock in data and raise license fees; being a different context is no reason to avoid reuse."@en .

<#q-public-code-r03-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Reuse it, but only by asking the first municipality's vendor to install it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Because the code is open, any vendor or in-house team can work on it; no single vendor holds the key."@en .

<#q-public-code-r04>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A town reuses a city's public codebase for housing benefits, including the city's written eligibility policy. The town's law differs on one point. What does the Standard expect here?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The town adapts it locally; the Standard does not cover the town's law"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Public code is a building block others can re-implement in their local context, and the written policy is part of the codebase. The Standard does not tell implementers how to comply with their legal framework."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-public-code-r04-d1> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r04-d2> ,
                          <#q-public-code-r04-d3> .

<#q-public-code-r04-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Standard tells the town how to change the policy for its law"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Its limitation of scope says it does not tell implementers how to comply with their local legal framework."@en .

<#q-public-code-r04-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The town may reuse only the software, not the city's policy"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The codebase includes policy as well as source code, and both are there to be reused."@en .

<#q-public-code-r04-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The city must first rewrite the policy to fit the town's law"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Public code is a building block that others re-implement in their own local context; nothing in the Standard makes the city rewrite its policy to fit another town's law."@en .

# Chapter 2: Reading and applying the Standard.
<#ch-reading>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    dcterms:title "Reading and applying the Standard"@en ;
    dcterms:description "How every criterion is laid out, who its role sections speak to, what the capitalized words MUST, SHOULD and OPTIONAL mean, and how a codebase is checked against the Standard. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-reading-r01> ,
                              <#q-reading-r02> .

<#ch-reading-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-reading> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## How a criterion is built

The Standard has 16 criteria, from “Code in the open” to “Document codebase maturity”. They are referred to by name, and nothing in the release says their order ranks their importance.

“All criteria have consistent sections”, so every criterion has:

- an introduction, explaining “what the criterion aims to achieve and why”
- **Requirements**, listing “what needs to be done in order to comply with the standard”
- **How to test**
- one “what you need to do” section each for public policy makers, managers, and developers and designers

Each criterion page also ends with a **Further reading** section.

How to test “offers actions you can take to see if a contribution is compliant”. It is worded so that “someone who is not intimately acquainted with the subject matter can still do a basic check for compliance”.

---

The three role sections offer “concrete actions” for each role:

- **Public policy makers** “set the priorities and goals of projects and may be less technologically experienced”.
- **Managers** are “responsible for on-time project delivery, stakeholder management and continued delivery of the service”, and they need to “line up the right resources”.
- **Developers and designers** “are usually more technically aligned and have more impact on the delivery of services than the previous groups”.

These roles are about what you do, not what you are called. They “apply to anyone performing duties associated with these roles, regardless of their job title”, and one person often has duties that span several roles."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-reading-1a> ,
                         <#q-reading-1b> .

<#q-reading-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A manager with no software background is asked to do a first check of whether a codebase meets one criterion. Which section of the criterion is written for exactly that?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The How to test section"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "How to test lists actions for checking compliance, worded so that someone not deeply familiar with the subject can still do a basic check."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reading-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-reading-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-reading-1a-d3> .

<#q-reading-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The Requirements section"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Requirements say what must be done to comply; the actions for checking it are in How to test."@en .

<#q-reading-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The introduction to the criterion"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The introduction explains what the criterion aims for and why it matters, not how to check it."@en .

<#q-reading-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The section for managers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That section gives managers actions for their own role, not a compliance check."@en .

<#q-reading-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Ada's job title at a regional agency is “architect”, but her work is managing stakeholders, lining up staff and answering for on-time delivery. Which section of each criterion speaks to these duties?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The section for managers"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The roles apply to anyone doing the duties, whatever their job title. The readers' guide makes managers responsible for on-time delivery and stakeholder management, and has them line up the right resources."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reading-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-reading-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-reading-1b-d3> .

<#q-reading-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The section for developers and designers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Roles follow duties, not job titles. Stakeholders, staffing and delivery are manager duties."@en .

<#q-reading-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The section for public policy makers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Policy makers set priorities and goals; delivering on time with the right resources is the managers' job."@en .

<#q-reading-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The section for vendors"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no vendor section. The role sections are for policy makers, managers, and developers and designers, and they apply to anyone doing those duties, whatever their job title."@en .

<#ch-reading-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-reading> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## MUST, SHOULD and OPTIONAL

The readers' guide says: “The following keywords in this document are to be interpreted as described in IETF RFC 2119”. The list is MUST, MUST NOT, REQUIRED, SHALL, SHALL NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, RECOMMENDED, MAY and OPTIONAL.

The Standard does not restate what these words mean, so you take their meanings from RFC 2119 itself. The strongest pair:

- **MUST** means “an absolute requirement of the specification”.
- **MUST NOT** means “an absolute prohibition of the specification”.

An absolute requirement leaves no room for exceptions: if a requirement says MUST and the codebase does not do it, the requirement is not met.

---

**SHOULD** is softer, but it is not a free choice. It means that “there may exist valid reasons in particular circumstances to ignore a particular item, but the full implications must be understood and carefully weighed before choosing a different course”. SHOULD NOT mirrors it, for something you avoid unless you have such reasons.

**MAY** and **OPTIONAL** mean that “an item is truly optional”: leaving it out needs no reason at all. The criteria use OPTIONAL for welcome extras, such as “Regular presentations at conferences by the community”.

One criterion often mixes all three levels. In “Code in the open”:

- source code for software in use MUST be published, unless it is used for fraud detection
- source code not currently in use SHOULD be published
- documenting which source code underpins an interaction the public has with an organization is OPTIONAL"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-reading-2a> ,
                         <#q-reading-2b> .

<#q-reading-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A requirement says reviews SHOULD happen within two business days. A team's reviews sometimes take four days when reviewers are on leave. Under RFC 2119, which the Standard uses, what makes this in line with the requirement?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A valid reason, with its implications understood and weighed"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "SHOULD lets an item be set aside in particular circumstances, but only for valid reasons whose full implications have been understood and carefully weighed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reading-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-reading-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-reading-2a-d3> .

<#q-reading-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, since only reviews within two days comply"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That would make it a MUST, an absolute requirement. SHOULD leaves room for exceptions that have been weighed."@en .

<#q-reading-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing extra, since a SHOULD item is truly optional"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Truly optional is MAY or OPTIONAL. A SHOULD needs a valid reason and a weighed decision before it is set aside."@en .

<#q-reading-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keeping late reviews to fewer than half of all the team's reviews"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "SHOULD sets no quota. What it asks for is a valid reason for each departure, with its implications weighed."@en .

<#q-reading-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A small team's codebase has never been presented at a conference, and the criterion marks regular presentations at conferences by the community as OPTIONAL. What does that mean for a review of the codebase?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Nothing is missing, since the item is truly optional"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Under RFC 2119, OPTIONAL (like MAY) means an item is truly optional. Leaving it out needs no reason and is no shortfall in a review."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reading-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-reading-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-reading-2b-d3> .

<#q-reading-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The team must give a weighed reason for leaving it out"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Weighed reasons are what SHOULD asks for. OPTIONAL asks for nothing at all."@en .

<#q-reading-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The criterion is unmet until the team gives a talk"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A truly optional item cannot leave a criterion unmet. It is a welcome extra."@en .

<#q-reading-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The team should avoid talks, as with a SHOULD NOT"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "SHOULD NOT advises against something. OPTIONAL neither asks for an item nor discourages it."@en .

<#ch-reading-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-reading> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Checking a codebase against the Standard

The release ships a **review template** for codebase review: a form for recording a verdict on every requirement. For each criterion it has a “criterion met” checkbox and a table with the columns “Meets”, “Requirement” and “Notes and links”, which lists every requirement of that criterion.

A review names the version of the Standard it checks against. The template is headed “________ and the Standard for Public Code version 0.8.1”.

For a lighter touch there is also a compact checklist of the requirements, made for “printed use and in-person discussions”.

---

To see how ready your codebase is, a quick **eligibility self assessment** gives you “a rough idea of how much work you may need to do to meet all criteria”.

If your codebase community wants to show that it is working toward the Standard, the README invites it to advertise “the codebase community's aspiration to meet the criteria”: link “the documentation of this commitment” from the standard-for-public-code-commitment badge. The badge signals commitment, not compliance.

Help is at hand while you apply the Standard. “For inspiration, look at the community built implementation guide which contains examples and other tips.” If anything in the Standard is unclear, the README invites you to open an issue. Translations into other languages exist too, but they are “Unofficial community translations”."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-reading-3a> ,
                         <#q-reading-3b> .

<#q-reading-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A municipality has just started working toward the Standard and wants to show this on its repository page. What does the Standard offer for that?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A commitment badge linked to its documented aspiration"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The README invites a codebase community to link the documentation of its commitment from the commitment badge. It advertises an aspiration to meet the criteria, not that they are met."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reading-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-reading-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-reading-3a-d3> .

<#q-reading-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A compliance badge awarded after a first review"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The badge the Standard offers signals commitment, not compliance, and needs no review first."@en .

<#q-reading-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An official certificate for each criterion met"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The Standard offers no certificate for each criterion met. To show that work toward the Standard has begun, it offers the commitment badge."@en .

<#q-reading-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A place in a public register of compliant codebases"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No such register is part of the Standard. What it offers for this is the commitment badge."@en .

<#q-reading-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A team is reviewing its codebase against the Standard and needs a record of its verdict on every requirement. Which tool from the release is made for this?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The criteria review template"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The release ships the review template for codebase review. It lists every requirement of each criterion, with room for notes and links."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reading-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-reading-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-reading-3b-d3> .

<#q-reading-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The compact checklist"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The checklist is meant for printed use and in-person discussions, not a review's record of its verdicts."@en .

<#q-reading-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The eligibility self assessment"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That gives a rough idea of the work ahead, not a requirement-by-requirement record."@en .

<#q-reading-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each criterion's How to test section"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "How to test gives the actions for checking a requirement. It is not where the review's verdicts are recorded."@en .

<#q-reading-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Priya fills in the review template for a codebase. For the requirement that source code for software in use MUST be published, she follows the criterion's How to test and finds one module in use, not used for fraud detection, that is unpublished. The team points to the commitment badge on its repository. What should she record?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Not met, since a MUST allows no weighed exceptions"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "How to test gives the actions for checking. The template records each requirement, and under RFC 2119 a MUST is absolute, so an unpublished module in use leaves it unmet."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reading-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-reading-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-reading-r01-d3> .

<#q-reading-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Met, since the commitment badge shows the intent to publish"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The badge advertises an aspiration to meet the criteria. It satisfies no requirement."@en .

<#q-reading-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Met, if the team has weighed the cost of keeping it private"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Weighed exceptions belong to SHOULD. A MUST is an absolute requirement."@en .

<#q-reading-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Left open until an eligibility self assessment is done"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The self assessment only gives a rough idea of the work ahead. It decides nothing about single requirements."@en .

<#q-reading-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Ingrid, a policy maker, first wants a rough idea of how much work her city's codebase needs to meet all criteria, and then concrete actions she can take in her own role. Which two parts of the Standard's material should she turn to?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The eligibility self assessment, then the policy makers' sections"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The self assessment gives “a rough idea of how much work you may need to do to meet all criteria”, and the policy makers' section of each criterion offers concrete actions for that role."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reading-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-reading-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-reading-r02-d3> .

<#q-reading-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The review template, then the policy makers' sections"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The review template records a verdict on every requirement; the rough idea of the work ahead comes from the self assessment."@en .

<#q-reading-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The eligibility self assessment, then each How to test section"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "How to test gives actions for checking compliance; the actions for her own role are in the policy makers' sections."@en .

<#q-reading-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The commitment badge, then the sections for managers and developers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The badge advertises an aspiration and measures nothing, and her duties of setting priorities and goals are the policy makers' part."@en .

# Chapter 3: Code in the open, policy bundled.
<#ch-open-code>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    dcterms:title "Code in the open, policy bundled"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The first two criteria: publishing the source code of software and policy in use, keeping sensitive information out of the files and their history, and bundling policy with the source code that implements it. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-open-code-r01> ,
                              <#q-open-code-r02> .

<#ch-open-code-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-open-code> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Code in the open

The first criterion asks a public organization to work where everyone can see. Coding in the open “improves transparency, increases source code quality, makes the source code easier to audit, and enables collaboration”. It also helps citizens understand “how software and policy impact their interactions with a public organization”.

As chapter 2 previewed, the core requirement is short: “All source code for any software in use (unless used for fraud detection) MUST be published and publicly accessible.” The same holds for all source code for any policy in use, with the same exception.

---

What does “publicly accessible” mean in practice? The How to test section is precise: the source of each version in use must be “published on the internet where it can be seen from outside the original contributing organization and without the need for any form of authentication or authorization”. So a login of any kind, even a free one, fails the test.

The criterion also looks beyond what is running today. As you saw in chapter 2, “Any source code not currently in use (such as new versions, proposals or older versions) SHOULD be published.” That is a recommendation, not a requirement. Going one step further, documenting which source code or policy underpins a specific interaction the general public has with the organization is OPTIONAL.

Openness is not only a task for developers. Policy makers are asked to “Develop policies in the open”, and managers to “Collaborate with external vendors and freelancers by working in the open”."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-open-code-1a> ,
                         <#q-open-code-1b> .

<#q-open-code-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A city publishes the source code of its parking-permit software on a portal where anyone can read it after creating a free account. Does this meet Code in the open?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, it must be readable without any login"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "How to test asks that the source be visible from outside the organization without any form of authentication or authorization, and a free account is still authentication."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-open-code-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-open-code-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-open-code-1a-d3> .

<#q-open-code-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, since the account costs nothing"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Cost is not the test: any login fails “without the need for any form of authentication”."@en .

<#q-open-code-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, if the parking policy is published too"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Publishing the policy does not change the test for the software: its source must be visible without any form of authentication."@en .

<#q-open-code-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, it must be hosted on a government domain"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The criterion does not say where code is hosted, only that anyone can see it without logging in."@en .

<#q-open-code-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A social services department has three codebases: an eligibility engine in daily use, a fraud-detection module in use, and an old version kept only as an archive. Which must it publish?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Only the eligibility engine, which is in use"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Code in use MUST be published unless it is used for fraud detection. Code not in use, such as older versions, SHOULD be published, but that is not a MUST."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-open-code-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-open-code-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-open-code-1b-d3> .

<#q-open-code-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "All three, since every codebase must be published"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Fraud detection is exempt, and publishing code that is not in use is only a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-open-code-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The eligibility engine and the archived old version"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The old version is not in use, so publishing it is a SHOULD, not a MUST."@en .

<#q-open-code-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The eligibility engine and the fraud-detection module"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Code used for fraud detection is explicitly excepted from the publication requirement."@en .

<#ch-open-code-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-open-code> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Keeping sensitive information out

Publishing everything raises an obvious worry: what about private data? Code in the open answers with a firm rule: “The codebase MUST NOT contain sensitive information regarding users, their organization or third parties.”

Notice how far that reaches. The How to test section says: “Confirm that the codebase files and commit history do not include sensitive information.” The history counts, not just the latest files. If a secret is committed and then deleted in a later commit, it is still there in the history, and the codebase still fails the test.

---

So how do teams stay on the right side of the rule? The criterion gives developers and designers two habits.

- **Review every commit.** As a reviewer, check each commit for sensitive information “such as configurations, usernames or passwords, public keys or other real credentials used in production systems”.
- **Separate data from code.** “Clearly split data and source code, in order to meet the requirement about sensitive information.”

Put together, the answer to sensitive data is to keep it out of the code, not to keep the code out of sight. The only exception the criterion names for publishing code in use is fraud detection. Sensitive data is a reason to clean up the codebase, never a reason to leave it unpublished."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-open-code-2a> ,
                         <#q-open-code-2b> .

<#q-open-code-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A developer committed a production database password, then deleted it in the next commit. The current files are clean. Does the codebase now meet the sensitive-information requirement?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, the commit history must be free of it as well"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "How to test checks both the codebase files and the commit history for sensitive information, so a password that was ever committed still counts against the codebase."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-open-code-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-open-code-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-open-code-2a-d3> .

<#q-open-code-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, since only the current files are checked"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The test explicitly covers the commit history, not only the latest files."@en .

<#q-open-code-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, if the deleting commit explains why it was made"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A commit message that explains the change is asked for by Maintain version control, but it does not remove the password. The test checks the files and the commit history, and the password is still in the history."@en .

<#q-open-code-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, the database password must first be listed in the README"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Documenting a secret does not make it acceptable. The codebase and its history must not contain it at all."@en .

<#q-open-code-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A team is about to publish its housing-allocation app. The repository holds the code, a folder of real applicant records and a config file with production credentials. What does Code in the open advise the developers to do?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Keep the records and credentials apart from the source code"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Developers are told to split data and source code clearly, so the code can be published without carrying user data or real credentials along with it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-open-code-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-open-code-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-open-code-2b-d3> .

<#q-open-code-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Encrypt the records and credentials inside the repository"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Encrypted data is still data kept in the codebase. The advice is to split data from code, not to hide it inside."@en .

<#q-open-code-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Publish only the modules that never touch the records"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "All source code in use MUST be published, so leaving modules out breaks that rule."@en .

<#q-open-code-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Publish compiled binaries in place of the source"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The criterion requires the source code itself to be published, not binaries in its place."@en .

<#ch-open-code-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-open-code> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Bundle policy and source code

The second criterion, Bundle policy and source code, keeps code and the policy behind it together. “Understanding the domain and policies within that domain is fundamental to understanding what problems a codebase is trying to solve and how it sets out to solve them.” An organization weighing reuse must also understand “what process changes it must choose to make or how to contribute additional configurability” to fit the code to its own context.

Two MUSTs follow, in both directions:

- “The codebase MUST include the policy that the source code is based on.”
- “If a policy is based on source code, that source code MUST be included in the codebase, unless used for fraud detection.”

To test this, “Confirm with a civil servant” that all the policy the code is based on, and all the code the policy is based on, is included.

---

Two further practices are recommended. “Policy SHOULD be provided in machine-readable and unambiguous formats”, and continuous integration tests “SHOULD validate that the source code and the policy are executed coherently”.

Policy makers provide the policy texts for the repository and, “if the text is not available in English, also provide an English summary”. They “Track policy with the same version control and documentation used to track source code”.

Relevant policy includes legal obligations such as the EU's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) or the EU Web Accessibility Directive, and human rights policies, “like a public organization's commitment to equal opportunity”."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-open-code-3a> ,
                         <#q-open-code-3b> .

<#q-open-code-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A city publishes its benefit-calculation software openly, with no login, but the regulation those calculations implement is kept only on a separate council website. Which MUST does the codebase fail?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The policy the code is based on, inside the codebase"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The codebase MUST include the policy the source code is based on. A document held elsewhere, however public, does not put the policy in the codebase."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-open-code-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-open-code-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-open-code-3a-d3> .

<#q-open-code-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Publication of the software without any login"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "This MUST is already met: the software is published. What is missing is the policy inside the codebase."@en .

<#q-open-code-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Machine-readable policy in an unambiguous format"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Machine-readable, unambiguous policy is a SHOULD, not a MUST. The MUST broken here is that the regulation the code is based on is not in the codebase."@en .

<#q-open-code-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "CI tests that check the code and policy run coherently"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CI tests for coherent execution of code and policy are a SHOULD, not a MUST. The MUST broken here is that the policy the code is based on is not in the codebase."@en .

<#q-open-code-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A province writes its eligibility rules in a machine-readable decision notation, and its CI tests check that the software follows them. What level does the Standard set for these two practices?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "SHOULD: both are recommended"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Machine-readable, unambiguous policy and CI tests for coherent execution of code and policy are both SHOULDs. The MUSTs are to include the policy and the code it depends on."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-open-code-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-open-code-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-open-code-3b-d3> .

<#q-open-code-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "MUST: both are required for bundling"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The MUSTs require including policy and code. The formats and the tests are SHOULDs."@en .

<#q-open-code-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "SHOULD for the notation, MUST for the tests"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "CI tests that check code and policy run coherently are a SHOULD, the same level as machine-readable policy."@en .

<#q-open-code-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "MUST for the notation, SHOULD for the tests"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Machine-readable policy is a SHOULD, the same level as the CI tests."@en .

<#q-open-code-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Sara's municipality publishes the source code of its school-place ranking software openly, with no login. A configuration file in it holds the production database password. The council policy the code applies is written as plain prose and sits on a staff-only wiki. Older versions are not published. Which two things break a MUST or MUST NOT?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The password in the code, and the policy outside the codebase"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Code in the open forbids sensitive information in the codebase, and Bundle policy and source code requires the policy the code is based on to be in the codebase. Publishing older versions is only a SHOULD."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-open-code-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-open-code-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-open-code-r01-d3> .

<#q-open-code-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The unpublished older versions, and the password in the code"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The password breaks a MUST NOT, but publishing code that is not in use is only a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-open-code-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The unpublished older versions, and the policy on the wiki"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The policy breaks a MUST, but publishing code that is not in use is only a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-open-code-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The password in the code, and the policy written as plain prose"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The password breaks a MUST NOT, but machine-readable policy is only a SHOULD. The MUST broken is that the policy is not in the codebase."@en .

<#q-open-code-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A tax office's published codebase holds its tax-relief regulation and the calculator that implements it. The regulation is also based on a fraud-scoring script, which is left out of the codebase. May the script be left out, and how does How to test say an auditor confirms that nothing else required is missing?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes, the fraud exception covers it; confirm the rest with a civil servant"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Source code a policy is based on MUST be in the codebase “unless used for fraud detection”, the same exception as for publishing code in use. How to test asks to “Confirm with a civil servant” that all policy and source code is included."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-open-code-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-open-code-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-open-code-r02-d3> ,
                          <#q-open-code-r02-d4> .

<#q-open-code-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, the script must be included; confirm the rest with a civil servant"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Source code a policy is based on must be included unless it is used for fraud detection, which this script is."@en .

<#q-open-code-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The script must be published openly, but outside the codebase"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Code used for fraud detection is excepted from the publication requirement of Code in the open too."@en .

<#q-open-code-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, the fraud exception covers it; the auditor reads the commit messages"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Commit messages do not show what is included. How to test asks to “Confirm with a civil servant” that all policy and source code is included."@en .

<#q-open-code-r02-d4>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, the script must be included; the auditor reads the commit messages"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The fraud exception covers this script, so it may stay out. And commit messages do not show what is included: How to test asks to “Confirm with a civil servant” that all policy and source code is included."@en .

# Chapter 4: Reusable and portable.
<#ch-reusable>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    dcterms:title "Reusable and portable"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Criterion 3: building a codebase for other contexts, adapting it through configuration, staying independent of secret, proprietary and single-vendor dependencies, and sharing it between parties. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-reusable-r01> ,
                              <#q-reusable-r02> .

<#ch-reusable-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-reusable> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Built for other contexts

Reusable and portable code lets others “reuse what has been developed, test it, improve it and contribute those improvements back, leading to better quality, cheaper maintenance and higher reliability.”

The first requirement of Make the codebase reusable and portable sets the bar: “The codebase MUST be developed to be reusable in different contexts.” As chapter 1 showed, two contexts are different if they are different public organizations or different departments with no one decision maker who could make collaboration happen naturally.

How to test suggests asking a peer: “Confirm with someone in a similar role at another organization if they can use the codebase and what that would entail.”

For developers and designers, this means designing source:

- “for reuse by other users and organizations regardless of locale”,
- “to solve a general problem instead of a specific one”,
- “in logically meaningful and isolated modules”.

---

Three SHOULDs help a codebase travel. “Configuration SHOULD be used to make source code adapt to context specific needs”, so each organization sets its own values without changing the code. “The codebase SHOULD be localizable.” And modules SHOULD be documented “in such a way as to enable reuse in codebases in other contexts.”

One thing should stay out: “Source code and its documentation SHOULD NOT contain situation-specific information.” Reviewers check each commit for “situation-specific data such as hostnames, personal and organizational data, or tokens and passwords.”"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-reusable-1a> ,
                         <#q-reusable-1b> .

<#q-reusable-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Three municipalities share a parking-permit codebase, but each has its own zone names and fees. What does Make the codebase reusable and portable recommend?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Use configuration so the shared code adapts to each city"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Configuration SHOULD be used to adapt source code to context-specific needs, which keeps one codebase usable by all three municipalities without editing the code for each."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reusable-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-reusable-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-reusable-1a-d3> .

<#q-reusable-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keep a separate copy of the code for each city to edit"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Separately edited copies stop being one reusable codebase; configuration is the recommended route."@en .

<#q-reusable-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Write all three cities' zones and fees into the source"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Source code SHOULD NOT contain situation-specific information such as one city's zones and fees."@en .

<#q-reusable-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Make the other cities adopt one city's zones and fees"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The codebase must be reusable in different contexts, not force one context on the others."@en .

<#q-reusable-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "An assessor wants to check whether a codebase was really developed to be reusable in different contexts. What does How to test suggest?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Ask someone in a similar role elsewhere if they could use it"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "How to test asks you to confirm with someone in a similar role at another organization whether they can use the codebase and what that would entail."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reusable-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-reusable-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-reusable-1b-d3> .

<#q-reusable-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Count how often the latest release has been downloaded"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Download numbers are not part of the test; it asks a peer at another organization."@en .

<#q-reusable-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Confirm that the original organization has run it for a year"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Use by its own organization says nothing about reuse in other contexts."@en .

<#q-reusable-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Check that every module has been translated into English"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The test asks a peer elsewhere, not for translation. Language rules belong to Use plain English; this criterion asks only that the codebase be localizable."@en .

<#ch-reusable-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-reusable> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Independent of secret and proprietary dependencies

The second MUST of the criterion is about what the codebase depends on: “The codebase MUST be independent from any secret, undisclosed, proprietary or non-open licensed software or services for execution and understanding.”

Notice the last words. **Understanding counts as much as running**: if people need a closed tool just to make sense of the code, the codebase is not independent, even when it runs on open software alone.

How to test checks the running side directly: “Confirm that the codebase can run without using any proprietary or non open-licensed software or services.”

Why does this matter? Source code that “does not rely on the situation-specific infrastructure of any contributor, vendor or deployment can be tested by any other contributor.”

---

A related recommendation goes further: “The software SHOULD NOT require services or platforms available from only a single vendor.” How to test checks that the software can be deployed and run without them. Mind the levels: depending on a single vendor is a SHOULD NOT, but needing proprietary software to run or understand the code breaks a MUST.

Sometimes your own situation calls for a proprietary platform. That is allowed, with a condition: “If your context requires deploying to proprietary platforms or using proprietary components, make sure that collaborators can develop, use, test, and deploy without them.”

Finally, make sure the codebase documentation describes the build-time and runtime dependencies, so others can see what the code needs."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-reusable-2a> ,
                         <#q-reusable-2b> .

<#q-reusable-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A ministry's codebase runs only together with a closed-source database it licenses from one company. The team says this is acceptable, because depending on a single vendor is only a SHOULD NOT. What is wrong with their argument?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Needing proprietary software to run also breaks a MUST"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The single-vendor rule is indeed a SHOULD NOT, but the codebase MUST be independent of proprietary or non-open licensed software for execution, and this database is both."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reusable-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-reusable-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-reusable-2a-d3> .

<#q-reusable-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Single-vendor dependence is a MUST NOT, not a SHOULD NOT"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The single-vendor rule really is a SHOULD NOT; the breach of a MUST is the dependency on proprietary software."@en .

<#q-reusable-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The MUST covers only what is needed to understand the code"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Independence is required for execution and for understanding, so a database the code needs to run counts."@en .

<#q-reusable-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
