<#ch-reusable-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-reusable> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Shared by more than one party

Designing a codebase for reuse lets its mission, vision and scope be shared by multiple parties, and “Codebases developed and used by multiple parties are more likely to benefit from a self-sustaining community.”

The criterion makes three recommendations about sharing:

- “The codebase SHOULD be in use by multiple parties.”
- “The roadmap SHOULD be influenced by the needs of multiple parties.”
- “The development of the codebase SHOULD be a collaboration between multiple parties.”

None of these three is a MUST. The criterion's two MUSTs are that the codebase is developed to be reusable in different contexts, and that it is independent of secret and proprietary software, as in the previous step.

---

How to test depends on the codebase's stage:

- In “early development before a production-ready release”, check “for evidence of ambition to obtain collaborators.”
- If it is “very mature and stable with very infrequent fixes, patches, or contributions”, check that it is in use by multiple parties or in multiple contexts, and look for “documented and budgeted commitments of collaboration.”
- Otherwise, check that it is in use by multiple parties or in multiple contexts, and that its contributors are from multiple parties.

Each role can help. Policy makers make sure their organization “is listed as a known user by the codebase” and identify other organizations for their teams to collaborate with. Managers make sure that stakeholders and business owners understand “that reusability is an explicit codebase goal.”"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-reusable-3a> ,
                         <#q-reusable-3b> .

<#q-reusable-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A new codebase from one agency has no other users yet and no production-ready release. What does How to test look for on collaboration between parties?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Evidence of ambition to obtain collaborators"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "For a codebase in early development, before a production-ready release, the test asks only for evidence of ambition to obtain collaborators. Use by multiple parties is checked later."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-reusable-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-reusable-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-reusable-3a-d3> .

<#q-reusable-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "At least two organizations already using it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Use by multiple parties is checked for codebases past early development, not before a production-ready release."@en .

<#q-reusable-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Contributors from several parties in its history"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Contributors from multiple parties are checked for codebases that are neither early nor very mature."@en .

<#q-reusable-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Budgeted commitments signed by partner agencies"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Documented, budgeted commitments of collaboration are checked for very mature, stable codebases."@en .

<#q-reusable-3b>
