# Chapter 5: Welcoming contributors.
<#ch-contributors>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    dcterms:title "Welcoming contributors"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Criteria 4 and 5: who may suggest changes, contribution guidelines and governance, the commitments organizations advertise, and the issue trackers and channels, open and closed, that make contributing easy. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-contributors-r01> ,
                              <#q-contributors-r02> .

<#ch-contributors-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-contributors> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Welcome contributors

People choose between codebases partly by how it feels to take part: “The atmosphere in a codebase community helps users decide to use one codebase over another.” A community where contributors have clear ways to influence its goals “is less likely to split”, and “Newcomers need to understand and trust the codebase community's governance.”

Welcome contributors, the fourth criterion, starts with two MUSTs:

- “The codebase MUST allow anyone to submit suggestions for changes to the codebase.”
- The codebase MUST include contribution guidelines “explaining what kinds of contributions are welcome and how contributors can get involved, for example in a `CONTRIBUTING` file”.

---

Allowing anyone to suggest a change is not the same as accepting every suggestion. The MUST is that anyone can submit one; the codebase community still decides what it accepts.

A code of conduct is a common addition, but here it is explicitly optional: “Including a code of conduct for contributors in the codebase is OPTIONAL.” The criterion's How to test list does say “Check for a code of conduct”, and that can mislead a reader. A check in How to test helps you see what is there; it does **not** raise a requirement's level. An OPTIONAL item stays OPTIONAL.

Finally, notice the words “for example”. A file name such as `CONTRIBUTING` is a suggestion. What the criterion requires is the content: guidelines that tell people what is welcome and how to get involved."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-contributors-1a> ,
                         <#q-contributors-1b> .

<#q-contributors-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A ministry's codebase accepts suggestions for changes only from partner agencies that have signed an agreement. What does Welcome contributors require about who may suggest changes?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "That anyone can submit suggestions for changes"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Welcome contributors makes it a MUST that anyone can submit suggestions for changes. Whether a suggestion is then accepted is a separate matter, which the codebase community decides."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-contributors-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-contributors-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-contributors-1a-d3> .

<#q-contributors-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "That suggestions come from organizations using the codebase"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Using the code is not a condition: the MUST says anyone may submit suggestions."@en .

<#q-contributors-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "That every suggestion is accepted after a short review"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The MUST is about allowing anyone to make suggestions, not about accepting them."@en .

<#q-contributors-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "That suggestions come from staff of the owning ministry"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing limits suggestions to the owner's own staff; anyone must be able to submit them."@en .

<#q-contributors-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "An auditor marks a codebase as failing Welcome contributors because it has no code of conduct, pointing out that How to test checks for one. Is the auditor right?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, a code of conduct is OPTIONAL in this criterion"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The requirement says including a code of conduct is OPTIONAL. How to test checks whether one exists, but that does not turn an OPTIONAL item into a MUST."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-contributors-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-contributors-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-contributors-1b-d3> .

<#q-contributors-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, a code of conduct is a MUST, like contribution guidelines"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Contribution guidelines are a MUST; the code of conduct is explicitly OPTIONAL."@en .

<#q-contributors-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, since a How to test check makes an item required"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "How to test helps you check compliance; the requirement itself sets the level."@en .

<#q-contributors-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, but it fails a SHOULD that needs a weighed reason"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A code of conduct is OPTIONAL, not a SHOULD, so leaving it out needs no reason."@en .

<#ch-contributors-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-contributors> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Governance and commitment

Contribution guidelines say what is welcome; governance says who decides, and how. Welcome contributors makes that a MUST too: “The codebase MUST document the governance of the codebase, contributions and its community, for example in a `GOVERNANCE` file.”

The How to test list sets the bar: “Confirm that the codebase governance is clearly explained, including how to influence codebase governance.” Managers are asked to make sure the governance documentation “includes the current process for how to make changes to the governance”. So a newcomer should learn not only who decides today, but how that arrangement could change.

---

The criterion then adds four SHOULDs:

- the contribution guidelines document “who is expected to cover the costs of reviewing contributions”;
- the codebase advertises “the committed engagement of involved organizations” in development and maintenance;
- it has “a publicly available roadmap”;
- it publishes “codebase activity statistics”.

For committed engagement, How to test only checks for a list of involved organizations, but the guidance for managers asks for more: make sure the documentation “explains how each organization is involved in the codebase, what resources it has available for it and for how long”.

Developers and designers have a part too: “Respond promptly to requests”, and tell contributors clearly what they need to do for their contribution to be integrated."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-contributors-2a> ,
                         <#q-contributors-2b> .

<#q-contributors-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Newcomers to a codebase cannot find out who decides what gets merged, or how that arrangement could ever change. What is the codebase missing?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Documented governance, including how it can be changed"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The governance of the codebase, contributions and community MUST be documented, and How to test checks that it explains how to influence that governance."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-contributors-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-contributors-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-contributors-2a-d3> .

<#q-contributors-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A code of conduct describing expected behavior"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A code of conduct covers behavior, not decision making, and it is OPTIONAL."@en .

<#q-contributors-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A public roadmap listing the planned features"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A roadmap shows plans; it does not say who decides, or how that could change."@en .

<#q-contributors-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Contribution guidelines saying which changes are welcome"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Guidelines say what is welcome and how to get involved; who decides, and how that can change, is governance, a separate MUST."@en .

<#q-contributors-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Two agencies co-fund a shared codebase. What does Welcome contributors suggest the codebase document about their engagement?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "How each is involved, its resources, and for how long"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The codebase SHOULD advertise the committed engagement of involved organizations. The guidance for managers spells it out: the documentation explains how each is involved, what resources it has available and for how long."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-contributors-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-contributors-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-contributors-2b-d3> .

<#q-contributors-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Their total IT budgets and their staff numbers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Only the resources committed to this codebase matter, not the agencies' overall budgets."@en .

<#q-contributors-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Their names as sponsors, and the date each one joined"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "How to test checks for a list of involved organizations, but names and join dates do not say what each commits, or for how long."@en .

<#q-contributors-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Each agency's share of the original development cost"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Past spending says nothing about commitment going forward: what each organization brings now and for how long."@en .

<#ch-contributors-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-contributors> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Make contributing easy

The fifth criterion, Make contributing easy, starts from a simple observation: “The less effort it takes to make contributions that are accepted by the codebase, the more likely users are to become contributors.” It then asks for three things that make the way in visible, each a MUST:

- “a public issue tracker that accepts suggestions from anyone”;
- documentation that links “to both the public issue tracker and submitted codebase changes, for example in a `README` file”;
- “communication channels for users and developers, for example email lists”.

How to test checks that you can take part in a discussion with other users and developers through channels described in the codebase, so questions that end up in one developer's private inbox do not count.

---

Security problems are the exception to working in public. The criterion requires “a way to report security issues for responsible disclosure over a closed channel”, and documentation with “instructions for how to report potentially security sensitive issues”. Both parts matter: the channel keeps a flaw private until it can be handled, and the instructions tell the finder how to use it.

That makes **all five** requirements of Make contributing easy MUSTs.

The idea reaches beyond code. The guidance for policy makers says: “Track policy issues in the codebase, so that a relevant external policy expert can volunteer help.” Managers are asked to do the same for management issues, so that outsiders with relevant experience can step in."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-contributors-3a> ,
                         <#q-contributors-3b> .

<#q-contributors-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A resident finds a security flaw in a city's open source app. Where does Make contributing easy want such a flaw reported?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Over a closed channel, following documented instructions"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The criterion requires a closed channel for responsible disclosure and documented instructions for reporting security-sensitive issues, so the flaw is not public before it can be handled."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-contributors-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-contributors-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-contributors-3a-d3> .

<#q-contributors-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In the public issue tracker, like any other bug"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Security issues need a closed channel for responsible disclosure, not the public tracker."@en .

<#q-contributors-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In a private message to whichever maintainer they can find"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A private message is closed, but the criterion also requires documented reporting instructions, so a reporter need not guess whom to contact."@en .

<#q-contributors-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "In a public pull request that fixes the flaw"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A public fix discloses the flaw before it can be handled over a closed channel."@en .

<#q-contributors-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A team has a public issue tracker that accepts suggestions from anyone, and documented instructions for reporting security issues over a closed channel. Its `README` links only to that tracker, and questions go to one developer's private inbox. What does Make contributing easy still require of it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Links to submitted changes, and channels for users and developers"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The documentation MUST link to both the issue tracker and the submitted codebase changes, and there MUST be communication channels for users and developers."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-contributors-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-contributors-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-contributors-3b-d3> .

<#q-contributors-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Published activity statistics and a publicly available roadmap"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both are SHOULDs in Welcome contributors, not MUSTs of Make contributing easy."@en .

<#q-contributors-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A statement on who covers review costs, and a code of conduct"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Review costs are a SHOULD and a code of conduct is OPTIONAL; neither is missing as a MUST."@en .

<#q-contributors-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Advertised commitments from each organization involved"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Advertising committed engagement is a SHOULD in Welcome contributors, not one of the MUSTs of Make contributing easy."@en .

<#q-contributors-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A ministry opens its grants codebase. Anyone can file suggestions in its public tracker, and its `README` links to the tracker, the submitted changes and a mailing list. It has a contributing guide, but no code of conduct, no public roadmap and no document on who decides on changes, and security issues are reported in the public tracker. Which two gaps break MUSTs?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No documented governance, and no closed security channel"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Documented governance is a MUST in Welcome contributors, and a closed channel for responsible disclosure of security issues is a MUST in Make contributing easy."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-contributors-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-contributors-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-contributors-r01-d3> .

<#q-contributors-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No code of conduct, and no publicly available roadmap"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A code of conduct is OPTIONAL and a roadmap is a SHOULD, so neither breaks a MUST."@en .

<#q-contributors-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No code of conduct, and no closed security channel"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The closed channel is a MUST, but a code of conduct is only OPTIONAL."@en .

<#q-contributors-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No publicly available roadmap, and no documented governance"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Documented governance is a MUST, but a public roadmap is only a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-contributors-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Two ministries co-fund a codebase. A volunteer developer wants to discuss ideas with other users, and to learn who decides what gets merged. What MUST the codebase offer her for each?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Communication channels and its documented governance"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Make contributing easy says the codebase MUST have communication channels for users and developers, and Welcome contributors says it MUST document the governance of the codebase, contributions and its community."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-contributors-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-contributors-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-contributors-r02-d3> .

<#q-contributors-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A public roadmap and published activity statistics"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Both are SHOULDs that show plans and activity; neither says where to talk or who decides."@en .

<#q-contributors-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A closed security channel and its documented governance"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The closed channel is for responsible disclosure of security issues, not for discussing ideas."@en .

<#q-contributors-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A ministry's private email address and the funding agreement"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A private address is not a channel for users and developers, and governance must be documented in the codebase, not in an agreement between funders."@en .

# Chapter 6: History and review.

<#ch-history-review>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    dcterms:title "History and review"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Criteria 6 and 7: keeping every file under version control, recording decisions in commit messages, and having every contribution reviewed by someone else, promptly and ideally from another context. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-history-review-r01> ,
                              <#q-history-review-r02> .

<#ch-history-review-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-history-review> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Maintain version control

Version control tracks changes to a codebase over time. It gives you “structured documentation of the history of the codebase”, lets developers “work on changes in parallel” and “helps future developers to understand the reasons for changes”.

Three requirements are mandatory:

- “All files in the codebase MUST be version controlled.”
- “All decisions MUST be documented in commit messages.”
- “Every commit message MUST link to discussions and issues wherever possible.”

The criterion's How to test list confirms “that all commit messages explain why the change was made”. As chapter 1 defined it, a codebase is code (both source and policy), tests and documentation, so policy documents and deployment scripts are covered too: “all files required to understand the code, build and deploy” belong in version control.

---

The codebase SHOULD be kept in a distributed version control system. Contribution guidelines SHOULD require contributors to group relevant changes in commits, and SHOULD encourage file formats whose changes “can be easily viewed and understood” in the version control system. Maintainers SHOULD “mark released versions of the codebase, for example using revision tags or textual labels”.

It is OPTIONAL for contributors to sign their commits and provide an email address, so future contributors can contact them.

If a policy change creates a new version of the codebase, policy makers make sure the documentation makes clear “what the policy change is” and “how it's changed the codebase”. Adding a new category of applicant to a codebase that manages granting permits is such a change."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-history-review-1a> ,
                         <#q-history-review-1b> .

<#q-history-review-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A team keeps its source code in Git, but its deployment scripts and policy documents live on a shared drive. What does Maintain version control say?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "All files in the codebase must be version controlled"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The MUST covers all files of the codebase, and a codebase includes policy and documentation. Everything needed to understand, build and deploy belongs under version control."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-history-review-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-history-review-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-history-review-1a-d3> .

<#q-history-review-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only source code needs to be version controlled"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The requirement says all files of the codebase, not only its source code."@en .

<#q-history-review-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Policy documents are exempt because they are not code"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Policy is part of the codebase, and every file in the codebase must be version controlled, not only the code."@en .

<#q-history-review-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Scripts and policy documents need only regular backups"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Backups are not version control, which records who changed what and why."@en .

<#q-history-review-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A city's team lists two version control MUSTs: all files in the codebase are version controlled, and all decisions are documented in commit messages. What is the third MUST?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Commit messages link to discussions and issues wherever possible"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The third MUST is that every commit message links to discussions and issues, with “wherever possible” built into it. Keeping the codebase in a distributed version control system and marking released versions are SHOULDs, and signing commits is OPTIONAL."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-history-review-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-history-review-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-history-review-1b-d3> .

<#q-history-review-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The codebase is kept in a distributed version control system"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Keeping the codebase in a distributed version control system is a SHOULD, not a MUST."@en .

<#q-history-review-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Released versions are marked with revision tags or textual labels"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Marking released versions, for example with revision tags or textual labels, is a SHOULD, not a MUST."@en .

<#q-history-review-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Contributors sign their commits and provide an email address"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Signing commits and providing an email address is OPTIONAL for contributors."@en .

<#ch-history-review-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-history-review> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Require review of contributions

Peer review of contributions “is essential for source code quality, reducing security risks and operational risks”. It also shapes how a team works together: knowing that all code was reviewed “discourages a culture of blaming individuals” and encourages a culture of focusing on solutions.

Three requirements are mandatory:

- “All contributions that are accepted or committed to release versions of the codebase MUST be reviewed by another contributor.”
- “Reviews MUST include source, policy, tests and documentation.”
- “Reviewers MUST provide feedback on all decisions to not accept a contribution.”

So a review covers more than the code, and a reviewer may say no, but never without telling the contributor why.

---

The Standard also recommends what a good review does. The review process SHOULD confirm that a contribution conforms to “the standards, architecture and decisions set out in the codebase”, and reviews SHOULD include “running both the software and the tests”.

How many reviewers? “Performing reviews by multiple reviewers is OPTIONAL.” One other contributor is enough to meet the MUST.

The role sections say the same. Policy makers institute “a 'four eyes' policy where everything, not just source code, is reviewed”, and managers make sure “no contributor is ever alone in contributing to a codebase”."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-history-review-2a> ,
                         <#q-history-review-2b> .

<#q-history-review-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A team asks whether the Standard requires more than one reviewer per contribution. What does Require review of contributions say?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "One other contributor is required; more is OPTIONAL"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Accepted contributions MUST be reviewed by another contributor, and the criterion states explicitly that performing reviews by multiple reviewers is OPTIONAL."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-history-review-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-history-review-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-history-review-2a-d3> .

<#q-history-review-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two reviewers are required for release versions"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The MUST asks for one other contributor; more reviewers are OPTIONAL."@en .

<#q-history-review-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Review is only a SHOULD, so it may be skipped"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Review by another contributor is a MUST for accepted contributions, not a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-history-review-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two reviewers are required whenever policy changes"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No rule raises the number of reviewers for policy; review simply covers policy too."@en .

<#q-history-review-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A maintainer closes a contributor's proposed change without a word because it does not fit the architecture. Which requirement does this break?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Reviewers must give feedback on every rejection"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Reviewers MUST provide feedback on all decisions not to accept a contribution. Rejecting a change for architecture reasons is fine; doing so silently is not."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-history-review-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-history-review-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-history-review-2b-d3> .

<#q-history-review-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Changes must be accepted if they pass the tests"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No requirement forces acceptance; reviewers may reject a contribution, as long as they give feedback."@en .

<#q-history-review-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Reviews must cover policy as well as source"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "That is a real requirement, but the scope of the review is not the problem here; the missing feedback is."@en .

<#q-history-review-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Rejections must be approved by a second reviewer"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Multiple reviewers are OPTIONAL, and nothing requires a rejection to be approved by anyone else."@en .

<#ch-history-review-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-history-review> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Prompt and independent review

Who reviews matters. “Contributions SHOULD be reviewed by someone in a different context than the contributor.” This is the different context you met in chapter 1: another public organization, or a department for which there is not one decision maker who could make collaboration happen naturally. A colleague under the same manager is the same context.

When the review happens matters too. “Reviews SHOULD happen within two business days.” The Standard explains why: “A policy of prompt reviews assures contributors of a guaranteed time for feedback”, which increases both the rate of delivery and contributor engagement.

---

The tools can help enforce review. “Version control systems SHOULD NOT accept non-reviewed contributions in release versions.” When testing the criterion, you check “for use of branch protection” in the version control system, which stops changes from reaching a release branch without a review.

Developers have their part as well. They ask other contributors to review their work, inside their own organization or outside it, and try to respond to others' review requests promptly, “initially providing feedback about the concept of the change”."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-history-review-3a> ,
                         <#q-history-review-3b> .

<#q-history-review-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Contributions to a regional agency's codebase are always reviewed by a colleague in the same team, under the same manager. What does the Standard recommend on top of that?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A reviewer from a different context"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Contributions SHOULD be reviewed by someone in a different context than the contributor: another public organization, or a department without one shared decision maker. A teammate under the same manager is the same context."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-history-review-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-history-review-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-history-review-3a-d3> .

<#q-history-review-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A second reviewer under the same manager"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Multiple reviewers are OPTIONAL, and a second teammate under the same manager shares the same decision maker, so the review is still from the same context."@en .

<#q-history-review-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A reviewer with a more senior job title"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Seniority is not mentioned; the recommendation concerns the reviewer's context."@en .

<#q-history-review-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The team's own manager as the reviewer"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The manager is the team's one decision maker, so a review by the manager stays in the same context; the recommendation is someone from another public organization or department."@en .

<#q-history-review-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Ines's team says every change is reviewed, but its version control lets anyone merge straight into the release branch. What does the Standard expect the team to set up?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Branch protection, so unreviewed changes are refused"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Version control systems SHOULD NOT accept non-reviewed contributions in release versions, and the criterion's tests look for branch protection, which enforces exactly this."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-history-review-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-history-review-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-history-review-3b-d3> .

<#q-history-review-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Signed commits, so every change has a known author"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Signing commits is OPTIONAL and lets future contributors contact past ones; it does not show that a change was reviewed."@en .

<#q-history-review-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Release tags, so each released version can be traced"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Tags mark released versions; they do not stop an unreviewed change from being merged."@en .

<#q-history-review-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A code of conduct, so contributors know what to expect"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A code of conduct is OPTIONAL and sets expectations for behavior; it does not block unreviewed merges."@en .

<#q-history-review-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Jonas's agency merges a policy update the morning after it was proposed. A reviewer from a partner municipality checked the source code, but nobody read the changed policy text. The commit message just says “fix”. Which requirements are broken?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Review must cover policy, and commits must record decisions"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Require review of contributions says reviews MUST include source, policy, tests and documentation. Maintain version control says all decisions MUST be documented in commit messages."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-history-review-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-history-review-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-history-review-r01-d3> .

<#q-history-review-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Two reviewers were needed, and the commit was unsigned"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Multiple reviewers and signed commits are both OPTIONAL, so neither breaks a requirement."@en .

<#q-history-review-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The review was too slow, and no release tag was set"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The review came within a day, well inside two business days, and marking released versions is a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-history-review-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The reviewer was from another organization, and the branch was unprotected"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A reviewer from another organization is the different context the Standard recommends, and nothing in the scenario says branch protection was missing."@en .

<#q-history-review-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Kofi's municipal team merges a change after a long discussion in an issue about why the old approach failed. A reviewer from another municipality approved it the same day. What do the criteria still expect of the commit message?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Record the decision and link the issue, since both are MUSTs"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "All decisions MUST be documented in commit messages, and every commit message MUST link to discussions and issues wherever possible. The same-day review from another organization already meets the recommendations on context and timing."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-history-review-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-history-review-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-history-review-r02-d3> .

<#q-history-review-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Name the reviewer, since that proves a review happened"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No requirement asks the commit message to name the reviewer; it must record the decision and link to the discussion."@en .

<#q-history-review-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, since the issue already holds the whole discussion"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The decision must be documented in the commit message itself, with a link to the discussion."@en .

<#q-history-review-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Carry a signature, since signed commits are required"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Signing commits is OPTIONAL in Maintain version control, so a missing signature breaks nothing."@en .

# Chapter 7: Objectives, documentation and language.
<#ch-documentation>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    dcterms:title "Objectives, documentation and language"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Criteria 8, 9 and 10: documenting what a codebase is for and how to install and use it, choosing authoritative languages that always include English, and writing in plain words. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-documentation-r01> ,
                              <#q-documentation-r02> .

<#ch-documentation-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-documentation> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Document codebase objectives

Objectives tell people what a codebase is for. They help stakeholders and contributors scope its development, and they let anyone decide quickly whether “this codebase, or one of its modules, is interesting for them now or in the future”.

The criterion Document codebase objectives starts with one firm requirement: the codebase MUST contain “documentation of its objectives, like a mission and goal statement, that is understandable by developers and designers so that they can use or contribute to the codebase”. Developers and designers are the audience that counts here.

Two lighter requirements build on it. The documentation SHOULD “clearly describe the connections between policy objectives and codebase objectives”. And documenting the objectives “for the general public” is OPTIONAL: welcome, but not required.

---

The Standard asks each role to add its own part of the objectives to the documentation, “for example in the `README`”:

- **Policy makers** add the policy objectives, with links or references to the supporting policy documents, which are bundled under the Bundle policy and source code criterion.
- **Managers** add “the organizational and business objectives”.
- **Developers and designers** add “the technology and design objectives”.

Together these answer the first questions a newcomer has: which policy does this code serve, what does the organization want from it, and what is it technically trying to be? When one role's part is missing, the picture is incomplete."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-documentation-1a> ,
                         <#q-documentation-1b> .

<#q-documentation-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A municipality writes a friendly mission statement for residents, but nothing that tells developers what the codebase is meant to do. Where does it stand on Document codebase objectives?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It covers the OPTIONAL part but misses the MUST"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Objectives MUST be documented so that developers and designers can use or contribute to the codebase. Objectives for the general public are an OPTIONAL extra, welcome but no substitute."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-1a-d3> .

<#q-documentation-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It complies, since objectives are meant for residents"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The required audience is developers and designers; the general public is an optional extra."@en .

<#q-documentation-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It breaks the Standard by writing for residents at all"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Writing objectives for the general public is allowed as an OPTIONAL extra; what is missing is the developer-facing statement."@en .

<#q-documentation-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It misses only the SHOULD on linking policy objectives"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The missing developer-facing objectives break the MUST, not only a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-documentation-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A codebase's README explains the policy it serves, with links to the policy documents, and its technical and design goals. Nothing says why the organization runs it or what it should achieve for the organization. Whose objectives are missing?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The managers' objectives"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Each role adds its own objectives: policy makers the policy ones, managers the organizational and business ones, developers and designers the technology and design ones. Here only the managers' part is missing."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-1b-d3> .

<#q-documentation-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The policy makers' objectives"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The policy objectives are already there, with links to the policy documents."@en .

<#q-documentation-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The developers' and designers' objectives"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The technical and design goals are already stated, and those are the developers' and designers' part."@en .

<#q-documentation-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The general public's objectives"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The general public is an OPTIONAL audience for the objectives, not a role that adds its own."@en .

<#ch-documentation-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-documentation> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Document the code

Good documentation helps people understand what the source code does and how to use it. It is “essential for people to start using the codebase”, and it makes contributing easier too.

Document the code has three MUSTs:

- “All functionality MUST be described in a clear language. The audience is those that understand the purpose of the codebase.”
- The documentation MUST describe “how to install and run the software”.
- It MUST contain “examples demonstrating the key functionality”.

Notice the audience of the first MUST: people who already understand what the codebase is for. A wider audience comes next.

---

The SHOULDs widen the circle. The documentation SHOULD contain:

- an overview “easy to understand by a wide audience”, including “the general public and journalists”;
- how to install and run “a standalone version” of the software, with “a test dataset if necessary”;
- “examples for all functionality”, not only the key ones;
- the key components or modules and their relationships, for example as “a high level architectural diagram”.

There SHOULD also be “continuous integration tests for the quality of the documentation”.

How do you know your documentation is clear? You do not judge it yourself. The test reads: “Confirm that other stakeholders find the documentation clear and understandable. Stakeholders should include professionals from other public organizations and the general public.” Managers get a yardstick of their own: “is the documentation good enough to persuade a manager at another public organization to use this codebase?”"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-documentation-2a> ,
                         <#q-documentation-2b> .

<#q-documentation-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A codebase describes all its functionality clearly and explains how to install and run it, but never shows how any of its main features is actually used. Which MUST is it missing?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Examples demonstrating the key functionality"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Examples of the key functionality are a MUST in Document the code. Examples for all functionality are a SHOULD on top of that."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-2a-d3> .

<#q-documentation-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Examples for every single feature it has"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Examples for all functionality are only a SHOULD; the MUST covers the key functionality."@en .

<#q-documentation-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "An overview written for the general public"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The overview for a wide audience is a SHOULD, not a MUST."@en .

<#q-documentation-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Instructions for a standalone install with test data"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Instructions for installing a standalone version, with test data if needed, are a SHOULD, not a MUST."@en .

<#q-documentation-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A team wants to know whether its documentation is clear enough. Whose judgment does Document the code look for?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Other public organizations and the general public"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Clarity is tested with stakeholders beyond the team: professionals from other public organizations and the general public. The codebase's own managers also ask whether the documentation would persuade a manager at another public organization to use it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-2b-d3> .

<#q-documentation-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The developers who wrote it, since they know it best"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The test asks other stakeholders, not the authors, whether the documentation is clear and understandable."@en .

<#q-documentation-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Colleagues in the same team who did not write it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "People inside the team share its assumptions; the test asks other public organizations and the public."@en .

<#q-documentation-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The team's own tech lead, who signs off on each release"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The test asks other stakeholders beyond the team, including professionals from other public organizations and the general public."@en .

<#ch-documentation-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-documentation> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Authoritative languages

The criterion Use plain English opens with a fact of life: English is “the de facto language of collaboration in software development”. But “some contexts require languages other than English”, so “a codebase can have a set of authoritative languages, including English”.

Three MUSTs follow:

- “The set of authoritative languages for codebase documentation MUST be documented.”
- “English MUST be one of the authoritative languages.”
- “All codebase documentation MUST be up to date in all authoritative languages.”

So a codebase may name another language as authoritative alongside English, never instead of it. And every authoritative language is kept current, not just the one the team writes in first.

---

Policy gets a rule of its own: “All bundled policy MUST be available, or have a summary, in all authoritative languages.” A summary is enough, but it has to exist in each of them.

Beyond the authoritative set, “Providing additional courtesy translations of any code, documentation or tests is OPTIONAL.” Courtesy translations are “best effort”, so they need not keep pace with the authoritative ones. When a codebase has both kinds, developers make clear “which category each translation belongs to”, so readers know which texts they can rely on.

Keeping several languages up to date takes work. That is why managers are asked to “Ensure there is staffing or budget to provide translation for authoritative languages”."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-documentation-3a> ,
                         <#q-documentation-3b> .

<#q-documentation-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A municipality in a bilingual region declares Finnish and Swedish as the authoritative languages of its codebase documentation, and documents that choice. What does the Standard still require?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Add English to the set of authoritative languages"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A codebase may have several authoritative languages, but English MUST be one of them. Finnish and Swedish can stay authoritative alongside it."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-3a-d3> .

<#q-documentation-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Choose just one of the two as the authoritative language"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Several authoritative languages are allowed; the set just has to include English."@en .

<#q-documentation-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Replace both with English as the only authoritative language"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Other authoritative languages may stay alongside English; the Standard does not ask to drop them."@en .

<#q-documentation-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing more, since the choice of languages is documented"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Documenting the set is required, but English must also be part of it."@en .

<#q-documentation-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A codebase's authoritative languages are English and French. Volunteers also keep a German translation, updated when they can. What does the Standard ask of these translations?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "English and French kept current, German labeled as courtesy"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "All documentation MUST be up to date in every authoritative language. Courtesy translations are OPTIONAL and best effort, and developers are asked to make clear which category each translation belongs to."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-3b-d3> .

<#q-documentation-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "All three kept equally up to date at every release"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Only authoritative languages must be kept up to date; courtesy translations may lag."@en .

<#q-documentation-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "German removed until volunteers can keep it as current as the others"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Courtesy translations are OPTIONAL and best effort, so they are allowed even when they lag behind."@en .

<#q-documentation-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the English text kept up to date at each release"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "French is authoritative too, so its documentation must also be current."@en .

<#ch-documentation-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-documentation> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Source code in English, documentation in plain words

For the code itself, Use plain English is strict: “All source code MUST be in English, except where policy is machine interpreted as code.” So when policy is written in a form the software executes directly, it may stay in its own language. The test accepts non-English source code that “is policy or terms with preceding explanations”.

Why does language matter so much? “Public sector information needs to be accessible to all its constituents.” Plain and simple language makes the code and what it does easier to understand for a wider variety of people. It pays off in translation too: language that is easy to understand “lowers the cost of creating and maintaining translations”.

---

The criterion also asks for plain words. There SHOULD be “no acronyms, abbreviations, puns or legal/language/domain specific terms in the codebase without an explanation preceding it or a link to an explanation”. The terms themselves are fine; what matters is that each one is explained first or linked to an explanation.

Documentation also “SHOULD aim for a lower secondary education reading level”, as the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines recommend.

Managers help by adding such terms to a glossary and linking to it from the places where they are used. They are also asked to be critical of documentation and proposals, with a simple rule of thumb: “If you don't understand something, others will probably also struggle with it.”"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-documentation-4a> ,
                         <#q-documentation-4b> .

<#q-documentation-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A codebase's documentation uses phrases like “LPA decision under s.12 GDO” throughout. What does Use plain English recommend?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Explain the terms before use, or link to an explanation"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Acronyms, abbreviations and legal or domain-specific terms SHOULD come with a preceding explanation or a link to one, so that a wider audience can follow the documentation."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-4a-d3> .

<#q-documentation-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ban every legal term, even where the rules depend on it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Such terms are allowed as long as each one is explained first or linked to an explanation."@en .

<#q-documentation-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keep them, since the readers are legal professionals"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Documentation should aim for a lower secondary education reading level, for a wide audience."@en .

<#q-documentation-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Collect them in a glossary at the end, without links"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A glossary helps only if the terms link to it; the SHOULD asks for a preceding explanation or a link."@en .

<#q-documentation-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A city's eligibility rules are written in a Dutch-language decision notation that its software executes directly; the rest of the source code uses English names. Is the Dutch allowed?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes, policy machine-interpreted as code is the exception"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "All source code MUST be in English, except where policy is machine interpreted as code, which is what these rules are. As bundled policy, they still MUST be available, or summarized, in every authoritative language, English included."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-4b-d3> .

<#q-documentation-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, Dutch belongs in the documentation, not in the code"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Source code must be in English except where policy is machine interpreted as code, which is exactly what these rules are."@en .

<#q-documentation-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, if Dutch is one of the authoritative languages"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Authoritative languages concern documentation; the source-code exception is for machine-interpreted policy."@en .

<#q-documentation-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only if the city has no English-speaking residents"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The exception depends on the code being machine-interpreted policy, not on who lives in the city."@en .

<#q-documentation-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Marta's codebase has a goal statement for developers, a clear description of every feature, install instructions and examples of its key features. English and Greek are its authoritative languages, and its benefit rules run as Greek-language policy code. For the new release, the Greek user guide was updated but the English one was not. Which gap breaks a MUST?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The English documentation is out of date"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "All documentation MUST be up to date in every authoritative language, and English is always one of them. The goal statement, feature description, install instructions and key examples meet the MUSTs of the other two criteria."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-r01-d3> .

<#q-documentation-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "There is no overview for the general public"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An overview for a wide audience is a SHOULD in Document the code, not a MUST."@en .

<#q-documentation-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The objectives are not written for residents"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Documenting the objectives for the general public is OPTIONAL, so their absence breaks no requirement."@en .

<#q-documentation-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The benefit rules are written in Greek, not English"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Policy that is machine interpreted as code is the exception to the rule that source code is in English."@en .

<#q-documentation-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A ministry documents its codebase in English and Spanish, both authoritative. A manager reading the overview cannot follow terms like “IAM” and “RBAC”. What do the criteria recommend?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Explain each term before use or link it, in both languages"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Such terms SHOULD come with a preceding explanation or a link to one, and documentation MUST be up to date in all authoritative languages. The manager's trouble is the signal: others will probably struggle too."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-documentation-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-documentation-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-documentation-r02-d3> .

<#q-documentation-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Explain each term before use, in the English version only"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "All documentation MUST be up to date in every authoritative language, so the Spanish text needs the same explanations."@en .

<#q-documentation-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keep the terms, since the overview is meant for technical staff"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The overview SHOULD be easy to understand by a wide audience, including the general public and journalists."@en .

<#q-documentation-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Ban such terms from both language versions of the overview"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Such terms are allowed when each is explained first or linked to an explanation."@en .

# Chapter 8: Open standards and continuous integration.
<#ch-standards-ci>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    dcterms:title "Open standards and continuous integration"@en ;
    dcterms:description "Criteria 11 and 12: exchanging data through open standards and recording any non-open ones, testing every contribution automatically before it is admitted, and integrating small parts often. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-standards-ci-r01> ,
                              <#q-standards-ci-r02> .

<#ch-standards-ci-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-standards-ci> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Use open standards

Open standards “guarantee access to the knowledge required to use and contribute to the codebase”. They also “enable interoperability between systems and reduce the risk of vendor lock-in”. As chapter 1 noted, the Standard takes its definition from the Open Source Initiative: an open standard is “any standard that meets the Open Source Initiative's Open Standard Requirements”.

The criterion Use open standards opens with three MUSTs:

- “For features of the codebase that facilitate the exchange of data the codebase MUST use an open standard” that meets those requirements.
- “Any non-open standards used MUST be recorded clearly as such in the documentation.”
- “Any standard chosen for use within the codebase MUST be listed in the documentation with a link to where it is available.”

---

A fourth requirement, a MUST NOT, limits non-open standards: “Any non-open standards chosen for use within the codebase MUST NOT hinder collaboration and reuse.” So a non-open standard is not banned outright. Outside data exchange you may use one, as long as it is recorded as non-open, listed with a link, and hinders no one.

Three SHOULDs follow. If no open standard exists, “effort SHOULD be put into developing one”. Open standards that are machine testable SHOULD be preferred over open standards that are not, and non-open standards that are machine testable SHOULD likewise be preferred over non-open ones that are not.

How to test looks for a list of the standards followed, each with a working link, “or a statement that no standards were chosen”. For public policy makers the advice is firm: “Prohibit procurement of technology that does not use open standards.”"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-standards-ci-1a> ,
                         <#q-standards-ci-1b> .

<#q-standards-ci-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A codebase sends case data to other agencies in a vendor's closed file format. Which criterion does this fail?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Use open standards"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Features that exchange data MUST use an open standard, one that meets the Open Source Initiative's Open Standard Requirements. A vendor's closed file format does not."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-standards-ci-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-1a-d3> .

<#q-standards-ci-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Publish with an open license"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Licensing concerns the codebase's own code and documentation, not the formats it uses to exchange data."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use plain English"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Use plain English concerns how the documentation and policy are written, not the formats the codebase uses to exchange data."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use continuous integration"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Continuous integration concerns the automated testing and frequent merging of contributions, not exchange formats."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A codebase uses one non-open standard for an internal report layout, and it hinders nobody's collaboration or reuse. What must the codebase's documentation do about it?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Record it clearly as non-open, and list it with a link"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Non-open standards are allowed outside data exchange if they do not hinder collaboration and reuse, but they MUST be recorded as non-open, and every chosen standard MUST be listed with a link."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-standards-ci-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-1b-d3> .

<#q-standards-ci-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Leave it out, since only open standards are listed"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Any standard chosen for use within the codebase must be listed, open or not."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Promise to replace it before the next release"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The criterion sets no deadline for replacing it; what it requires is recording and listing the standard."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "List it with a link, without noting it is non-open"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Listing is only half the rule: a non-open standard MUST also be recorded clearly as non-open in the documentation."@en .

<#ch-standards-ci-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-standards-ci> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Use continuous integration

Continuous integration (CI) is “the practice of merging all developers' working copies to a development branch of a codebase as frequently as reasonable”. The reason is practical: “The more frequent the merging and the smaller the contribution, the easier it is to resolve merge conflicts.”

Automated tests are the other half. Automated testing of all functionality gives confidence that contributions work as intended and have not introduced errors. It also lets “reviewers focus on the structure and approach of the contribution”.

---

The criterion Use continuous integration sets four MUSTs:

- “All functionality in the source code MUST have automated tests.”
- “Contributions MUST pass all automated tests before they are admitted into the codebase.” Fixing a failing test afterwards does not meet this.
- The codebase MUST have guidelines “explaining how to structure contributions”.
- It MUST have “active contributors who can review contributions”.

How to test makes two of these concrete. It asks you to confirm that source code coverage tools check that “coverage is at 100% of the source code”, and to look for “contributions from within the last three months” as the sign of active contributors.

Note where the 100% figure lives: in How to test, not in the requirements themselves."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-standards-ci-2a> ,
                         <#q-standards-ci-2b> .

<#q-standards-ci-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A team admits a contribution whose automated tests fail, planning to fix them next week. What does Use continuous integration say about this contribution?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It may be admitted only once all its tests pass"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Contributions MUST pass all automated tests before they are admitted into the codebase. Admitting it now and fixing the tests afterwards does not meet this requirement."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-standards-ci-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-2a-d3> .

<#q-standards-ci-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It may be admitted if a reviewer approves it first"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Review does not replace the tests: contributions MUST pass all automated tests before they are admitted."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It may be admitted if the fix follows within a week"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "There is no grace period; the tests must pass before admission, not afterwards."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It may be admitted, as only releases must pass tests"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The MUST covers every contribution admitted into the codebase, not just releases."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Hamid's codebase names five reviewers in its contributing guide, but its last contribution was merged eight months ago. Judged by the criterion's How to test, does the codebase meet the MUST to have active contributors who can review contributions?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It fails, as no one contributed in the last three months"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "How to test makes “active” concrete: it looks for contributions from within the last three months. Eight months without a single contribution fails that check, however many reviewers are named."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-standards-ci-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-2b-d3> .

<#q-standards-ci-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It passes, as five named reviewers are on record"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The test judges activity by recent contributions, not by a list of names."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It passes, as activity is checked only before each release"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The test has no release condition: it looks for contributions from within the last three months."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It fails, as it has fewer than ten reviewers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "No number of reviewers is set; what fails the test is the lack of recent contributions."@en .

<#ch-standards-ci-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-standards-ci> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Continuous integration in practice

Beyond its MUSTs, Use continuous integration recommends three things:

- “Automated test results for contributions SHOULD be public.”
- The codebase guidelines SHOULD state that “each contribution should focus on a single issue”.
- “Source code test and documentation coverage SHOULD be monitored.”

Three more are OPTIONAL in this criterion: testing policy and documentation for consistency with the source and vice versa, testing them “for style and broken links”, and testing the software “by using examples in the documentation”.

Other criteria set their own levels for continuous integration on their own subjects. Bundle policy and source code says CI tests SHOULD validate that source code and policy “are executed coherently” (chapter 3), and Document the code says there SHOULD be CI tests “for the quality of the documentation” (chapter 7). When you assess a codebase, check each requirement under its own criterion.

---

The criterion also tells each role what to do. Managers should “Plan the work to integrate small parts very often instead of large parts less frequently”, and “test with real end users as quickly and often as possible”. When things go badly wrong, the advice is openness: “After a large failure, encourage publication of incident reports and public discussion of what was learned.”

Developers and designers get matching advice:

- “Help contributors limit the scope of their contributions and feature requests to be as small as reasonable.”
- “Deploy often.”
- “Integrate your work at least once a day.”"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-standards-ci-3a> ,
                         <#q-standards-ci-3b> ,
                         <#q-standards-ci-3c> .

<#q-standards-ci-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A manager plans a two-year project with one big integration at the end. What does the managers' guidance in Use continuous integration advise instead?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Integrate small parts very often, not large parts rarely"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Managers are told to plan the work to integrate small parts very often instead of large parts less frequently; smaller, more frequent merges make conflicts easier to resolve."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-standards-ci-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-3a-d3> .

<#q-standards-ci-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Integrate at each project milestone, every few months"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Every few months is still large parts integrated rarely; the guidance asks for small parts very often, and developers integrate at least daily."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Integrate once, after user testing has ended"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The guidance asks for testing with real end users early and often, alongside frequent integration."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keep one integration at the end, but review the code weekly"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Reviewing an unmerged branch more often still leaves one big merge at the end; the guidance is to integrate often."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A city's online service suffers a major outage. What does the managers' guidance in Use continuous integration encourage afterwards?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Publishing incident reports and discussing lessons openly"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "After a large failure, managers are to encourage publication of incident reports and public discussion of what was learned, rather than keeping the lessons inside the team."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-standards-ci-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-3b-d3> .

<#q-standards-ci-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keeping the incident reports internal to protect public trust"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The guidance encourages publication of incident reports and public discussion, not keeping them internal."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Going back to large, rare releases to cut risk"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The criterion favors small, frequent integration; after a failure it asks for public learning, not a retreat to big releases."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Freezing all contributions until an audit ends"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Nothing in the criterion asks to stop contributions after a failure."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-3c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Dana's contribution guidelines explain how to structure contributions but say nothing about their scope, and contributors often bundle several unrelated fixes into one. What does Use continuous integration set here?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A SHOULD: guidelines should ask for one issue per contribution"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Guidelines explaining how to structure contributions are a MUST, which Dana has. That the guidelines state that each contribution should focus on a single issue is a SHOULD."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-standards-ci-3c-d1> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-3c-d2> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-3c-d3> .

<#q-standards-ci-3c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A MUST: each contribution may only ever fix a single issue"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The MUST is to have guidelines on structuring contributions; the single-issue statement is a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-3c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "OPTIONAL, like testing documentation for broken links"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Broken-link tests are OPTIONAL in this criterion, but the single-issue statement is a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-3c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing more, since guidelines on structure already exist"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "They meet the MUST, but the criterion also recommends that guidelines ask for one issue per contribution."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Hana's codebase uses a non-open standard for its print layout, recorded as non-open, listed with a link, and hindering no one's collaboration or reuse. Its CI results are visible only to staff, one module has no automated tests, and she integrates her work once a week. Which of these breaks a MUST?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Leaving one module without any automated tests"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "All functionality in the source code MUST have automated tests. Of the other three, public test results are a SHOULD, daily integration is developer guidance, and the non-open standard is used within the rules of Use open standards."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-standards-ci-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-r01-d3> .

<#q-standards-ci-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Showing the CI test results to staff only"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Public automated test results for contributions are a SHOULD, not a MUST."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Integrating her work weekly instead of daily"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Integrating at least once a day is guidance for developers, not a requirement of the criterion."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Using a non-open standard for the print layout"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A non-open standard is allowed outside data exchange when it is recorded as non-open, listed with a link, and hinders no one."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Tomás takes over a codebase that sends case files to other agencies in a vendor's closed format. Its team merges contributions in one large batch each quarter and fixes failing tests afterwards. Which plan follows Use open standards and Use continuous integration?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Exchange data in an open standard; merge small, tested parts often"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Features that exchange data MUST use an open standard; contributions MUST pass all automated tests before they are admitted; and managers plan “to integrate small parts very often instead of large parts less frequently”."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-standards-ci-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-standards-ci-r02-d3> .

<#q-standards-ci-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Keep the vendor format but list it; merge quarterly once tests pass"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Listing a non-open standard does not lift the MUST to use an open standard for data exchange, and quarterly batches are large parts integrated rarely."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Exchange data in an open standard; fix failing tests within a week"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Contributions MUST pass all automated tests before they are admitted, not within a week afterwards."@en .

<#q-standards-ci-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Exchange data in an open standard; review each quarterly batch closely"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Review does not replace passing tests before admission, and the guidance is to integrate small parts very often."@en .

# Chapter 9: License, findability, style and maturity.
<#ch-release>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    dcterms:title "License, findability, style and maturity"@en ;
    dcterms:description "The last four criteria: open licenses for code and documentation, making a codebase findable, following a style guide, and signaling which versions are ready to use. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-release-r01> ,
                              <#q-release-r02> .

<#ch-release-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-release> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Publish with an open license

An open, well-known license lets anyone see the source code, use it freely and contribute to it. In the Standard's words, this “enables a vendor ecosystem to emerge around the codebase”.

Three requirements are MUSTs:

- “All source code and documentation MUST be licensed such that it may be freely reusable, changeable and redistributable.”
- “Software source code MUST be licensed under an OSI-approved or FSF Free/Libre license.”
- “All source code MUST be published with a license file.”

One more is a MUST NOT: “Contributors MUST NOT be required to transfer copyright of their contributions to the codebase.”

---

Every source code file SHOULD also include “a copyright notice and a license header that are machine-readable”. Using several licenses for different types of source code and documentation is OPTIONAL.

How to test checks that the source code license is on the OSI-approved or FSF Free/Libre list, that the documentation license “conforms to the Open Definition”, and that “the licenses used in the codebase are included as files”. It also checks that neither the contribution guidelines nor the repository configuration demand a copyright transfer.

Be careful with documentation licenses. Creative Commons licenses suit documentation well, but “licenses that stipulate Non-Commercial or No Derivatives are NOT freely reusable, changeable and redistributable”, so they do not meet the first MUST.

Dependencies matter too: as chapter 4 showed, a codebase MUST be independent of non-open licensed software, so a dependency under such a license rules itself out."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-release-1a> ,
                         <#q-release-1b> ,
                         <#q-release-1c> .

<#q-release-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A city publishes its code under an OSI-approved license and its user manual under a Creative Commons Non-Commercial license. What does the Standard say?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The manual fails: its license is not freely reusable"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Documentation must be freely reusable, changeable and redistributable too. Licenses that stipulate Non-Commercial or No Derivatives do not fulfill that requirement."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-1a-d3> .

<#q-release-1a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The manual passes: Creative Commons licenses are all open"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Licenses that stipulate Non-Commercial or No Derivatives are named as not freely reusable, changeable and redistributable."@en .

<#q-release-1a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The manual passes: only source code needs an open license"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The MUST on free reuse, change and redistribution covers source code and documentation alike."@en .

<#q-release-1a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The code fails: it must share one license with the manual"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Using several licenses for different parts of a codebase is OPTIONAL, so it is allowed."@en .

<#q-release-1b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A municipality's contribution guidelines say every outside contributor must assign the copyright of their patch to the municipality, so it can relicense the code later. Is that allowed under the Standard?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, contributors may not be required to hand over copyright"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Publish with an open license forbids requiring contributors to transfer copyright (a MUST NOT), and its test checks both the contribution guidelines and the repository settings for such a demand."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-1b-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-1b-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-1b-d3> .

<#q-release-1b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, as long as the code stays under an OSI-approved license"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The license choice is a separate MUST; even a perfectly open license does not permit demanding a copyright transfer."@en .

<#q-release-1b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, since each file's copyright notice must name a single holder"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The per-file copyright notice is a SHOULD about marking files; it does not require one holder or allow a transfer demand."@en .

<#q-release-1b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only for patches large enough to need their own license header"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The MUST NOT has no size threshold: no contributor may be required to transfer copyright."@en .

<#q-release-1c>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "A team names its license in the `README` and puts a machine-readable copyright notice and license header in every source file, but the repository has no license file. Where does it stand?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "It misses a MUST, though it meets the SHOULD on headers"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Publishing source code with a license file is a MUST, and How to test checks that the licenses are included as files. Per-file headers are a separate SHOULD."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-1c-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-1c-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-1c-d3> .

<#q-release-1c-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It complies fully, since headers replace a license file"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Headers are recommended in addition to the license file, not instead of it."@en .

<#q-release-1c-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It misses only a SHOULD, since license files are recommended"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The license file is a MUST; it is the per-file headers that are a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-release-1c-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "It complies, since the `README` serves as the license file"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "How to test confirms that the licenses used in the codebase are included as files of their own."@en .

<#ch-release-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-release> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Make the codebase findable

The more findable a codebase is, the more collaborators find it, and “just publishing a codebase and hoping it is found does not work, instead proactiveness is needed”.

This is the only one of the 16 criteria with no MUST: every requirement is a SHOULD or OPTIONAL. The codebase SHOULD:

- have a name that is “descriptive and free from acronyms, abbreviations, puns or organizational branding”
- have a short description of what it is for or what it does
- be submitted to “relevant software catalogs”
- have a website that describes the problem it solves “using the preferred jargon of different potential users”
- be findable by a search engine both by its name and “by describing the problem it solves in natural language”

---

The name rules have sensible exceptions in How to test. An acronym is fine if it is “more universally known than the longer forms”, and organizational branding is fine when that organization “is of the codebase community itself”.

Two more SHOULDs make the codebase easier to refer to and discover through other tools. It SHOULD have “a unique and persistent identifier”, such as a Wikidata item, whose entry mentions the major contributors, the repository location and the website. And it SHOULD include “a machine-readable metadata description, for example in a `publiccode.yml` file”.

The rest is OPTIONAL: “A dedicated domain name” for the codebase and, as you saw in chapter 2, regular presentations at conferences by the community."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-release-2a> ,
                         <#q-release-2b> .

<#q-release-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A city builds a tool, meant for reuse by many other cities, that lets residents book a bulky-waste pick-up. Under Make the codebase findable, what kind of name SHOULD it get, and what SHOULD the name be free from?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "A descriptive name, free from acronyms, abbreviations, puns and organizational branding"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A name SHOULD be descriptive and free from acronyms, abbreviations, puns and organizational branding, so people looking for what the codebase does recognize it. Here no one city is the whole codebase community, so a city's branding does not fit the exception."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-2a-d3> ,
                          <#q-release-2a-d4> ,
                          <#q-release-2a-d5> ,
                          <#q-release-2a-d6> .

<#q-release-2a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Northbay City Waste Portal"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "One city's name is organizational branding; in a codebase shared by many cities, that city is not the whole codebase community, so the exception does not apply."@en .

<#q-release-2a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "BWPB Booking Tool"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An abbreviation that is not better known than its long form hides what the codebase does."@en .

<#q-release-2a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Bin There, Done That"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A pun is memorable, but puns are listed among the things a codebase name should avoid."@en .

<#q-release-2a-d4>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A short, catchy name, free from acronyms, abbreviations, puns and organizational branding"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The list of things to leave out is right, but the rule asks for a descriptive name, so people looking for what the codebase does recognize it."@en .

<#q-release-2a-d5>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A descriptive name, free from the users' jargon, domain names and persistent identifiers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The users' jargon belongs on the website, and a persistent identifier (a SHOULD) and a dedicated domain name (OPTIONAL) are separate items. The name SHOULD be free from acronyms, abbreviations, puns and organizational branding."@en .

<#q-release-2a-d6>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "A short, catchy name, free from the users' jargon, domain names and persistent identifiers"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The rule asks for a descriptive name. And it SHOULD be free from acronyms, abbreviations, puns and organizational branding, not from the users' jargon, domain names or identifiers."@en .

<#q-release-2b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "An assessor says a codebase without a `publiccode.yml` file fails a MUST of Make the codebase findable. Is that right?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "No, the criterion has only SHOULD and OPTIONAL items"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "A machine-readable metadata description is a SHOULD, and `publiccode.yml` is only an example of one; this criterion has no MUST at all."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-2b-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-2b-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-2b-d3> .

<#q-release-2b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, a `publiccode.yml` file is a MUST for every codebase"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The file is named only as an example of how to meet a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-release-2b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, some machine-readable metadata file is a MUST"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A machine-readable metadata description is a SHOULD in this criterion, not a MUST."@en .

<#q-release-2b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, a metadata file is OPTIONAL, like a domain name"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A dedicated domain name is OPTIONAL, but a machine-readable metadata description is a SHOULD."@en .

<#ch-release-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-release> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Use a coherent style

A consistent and coherent style “enables contributors in different environments to work together”, and “Unifying vocabularies reduces friction” in communication between them.

The criterion has one MUST: the codebase MUST use “a coding or writing style guide, either the codebase community's own or an existing one referred to in the codebase”. So you do not have to write your own guide. Adopting a well-known one is fine, as long as the codebase says which one it follows.

Note the word **writing**. Style is not only about source code: policy makers are asked to create, follow and keep improving a style guide “for policy and documentation” too, and to document it in the codebase.

---

Two SHOULDs build on the guide: “Contributions SHOULD pass automated tests on style”, and the guide SHOULD include “expectations for inline comments and documentation for non-trivial sections”. Including expectations for understandable English is OPTIONAL.

What if a codebase has no guidance yet? Developers and designers are advised to start by documenting “whatever is being done now”, for example in the `CONTRIBUTING` or `README` file. An important purpose of that document is to communicate “design preferences, naming conventions, and other aspects machines can't easily check”. Keep improving it until it grows into engineering guidelines.

For what machines can check, the advice is short: “Use a linter”, and add the linter configuration to the codebase."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-release-3a> ,
                         <#q-release-3b> .

<#q-release-3a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A small team never wrote its own style guide but follows a well-known existing one. Does it meet the MUST of Use a coherent style?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes, if the codebase refers to that existing guide"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The style guide may be the community's own or an existing one referred to in the codebase. The reference is what makes it the codebase's guide."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-3a-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-3a-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-3a-d3> .

<#q-release-3a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, the community must write a guide of its own"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An existing guide referred to in the codebase is explicitly allowed by the MUST."@en .

<#q-release-3a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, even if the guide is mentioned nowhere"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "An existing guide only counts when it is referred to in the codebase."@en .

<#q-release-3a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, the guide must also require plain English"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Expectations for understandable English in the style guide are OPTIONAL, not required."@en .

<#q-release-3b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A team's linter enforces formatting on every contribution. What should its written style guidance still communicate?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Design preferences, naming and other things machines can't check"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The written guidance exists to communicate design preferences, naming conventions and other aspects machines can't easily check; the linter covers the rest."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-3b-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-3b-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-3b-d3> .

<#q-release-3b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Nothing, since the linter configuration replaces it"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The linter is added alongside written guidance, which covers what machines can't easily check."@en .

<#q-release-3b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only the formatting rules the linter already enforces"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Repeating the linter misses the point; the written guidance covers what the linter cannot check."@en .

<#q-release-3b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The license terms under which outside contributions are accepted"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Licensing belongs to Publish with an open license, not to the style guide."@en .

<#ch-release-4>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-release> ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Document codebase maturity

Clearly signaling a codebase's maturity “helps others decide whether to use and contribute to it”. And maturity is not only about the codebase itself: “A codebase version's maturity includes the maturity of its dependencies.”

The criterion has three MUSTs:

- “The codebase MUST be versioned.”
- It “MUST prominently document whether or not there are versions of the codebase that are ready to use”.
- “Codebase versions that are ready to use MUST only depend on versions of other codebases that are also ready to use.”

Note what the second MUST asks for: a clear statement, not a finished product. A young codebase with no ready version yet meets this MUST, as long as it says so prominently.

---

Two SHOULDs help people follow how the codebase evolves. It SHOULD contain “a summary of changes from version to version, for example in the `RELEASE_NOTES`”, and “The method for assigning version identifiers SHOULD be documented.” Using semantic versioning is OPTIONAL, so any documented scheme can work.

“Prominently” has a test behind it: whether ready versions exist must be “obvious to policy makers, managers, developers and designers”, not only to the people who read the code.

Managers get a matching rule for the services they run: make sure “services only rely on versions of codebases of equal or greater maturity than the service. For example, don't use a beta version of a codebase in a production service.”"""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-release-4a> ,
                         <#q-release-4b> .

<#q-release-4a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A codebase marks its version 2.0 as ready to use, but 2.0 depends on a beta version of another codebase. Which criterion does this break?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Document codebase maturity"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Ready-to-use versions MUST only depend on ready-to-use versions of other codebases, because a version's maturity includes that of its dependencies."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-4a-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-4a-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-4a-d3> .

<#q-release-4a-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Use continuous integration"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Continuous integration concerns automated tests, not the maturity of dependencies."@en .

<#q-release-4a-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Maintain version control"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Version control is about tracking changes, not about which dependency versions a release may use."@en .

<#q-release-4a-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Make the codebase findable"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Findability concerns names, descriptions, catalogs and metadata, not the versions a release depends on."@en .

<#q-release-4b>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A team labels its releases by season and year, such as “2026-autumn”, rather than numbers like 1.2.0, and documents how it picks the labels. Does that meet what Document codebase maturity asks of versioning?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Yes, it is versioned, and semantic versioning is OPTIONAL"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "The codebase MUST be versioned and the method SHOULD be documented, but using semantic versioning is OPTIONAL. The other requirements, such as documenting readiness, still apply."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-4b-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-4b-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-4b-d3> .

<#q-release-4b-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, semantic versioning is a MUST for ready-to-use versions"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The criterion makes semantic versioning OPTIONAL for every version, ready to use or not."@en .

<#q-release-4b-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Yes, because being versioned at all is only a SHOULD"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Being versioned is a MUST; it is documenting the method that is a SHOULD."@en .

<#q-release-4b-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "No, labels cannot show whether a version is ready"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Readiness must be documented prominently, which any labeling scheme allows."@en .

<#q-release-r01>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:front "Leila prepares a first release. The code is under an OSI-approved license with a license file, the source files have no copyright or license headers, the codebase has no dedicated domain name, its versions are not numbered by semantic versioning, and its `README` says no version is ready to use yet. Which of these needs a weighed reason under the Standard?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "The missing license headers in the source files"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Machine-readable copyright and license headers are a SHOULD, so skipping them needs a weighed reason. The license file is in place, a domain and semantic versioning are OPTIONAL, and stating that no version is ready meets the maturity MUST."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-r01-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-r01-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-r01-d3> .

<#q-release-r01-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "The lack of a dedicated domain name"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A dedicated domain name is OPTIONAL, so leaving it out needs no reason."@en .

<#q-release-r01-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Having no version that is ready to use"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "The MUST is to document whether ready versions exist; saying that none does is compliant."@en .

<#q-release-r01-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Not using semantic versioning for the releases"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Semantic versioning is OPTIONAL; the MUST is only that the codebase is versioned."@en .

<#q-release-r02>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "A city's codebase depends on another city's library. The library's only version is marked as beta, and it is published under a Creative Commons No Derivatives license. The codebase now marks its 3.0 release as ready to use. What do the criteria say about that dependency?"@en ;
    solid-memo:back "Both its beta status and its license rule it out"@en ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Ready-to-use versions MUST only depend on ready-to-use versions. No Derivatives licenses are not freely reusable, changeable and redistributable, and a codebase MUST be independent of non-open licensed software, as chapter 4 showed."@en ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-release-r02-d1> ,
                          <#q-release-r02-d2> ,
                          <#q-release-r02-d3> .

<#q-release-r02-d1>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only its beta status; its license is the library's own concern"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "A non-open licensed dependency is the codebase's concern too: independence from such software is a MUST."@en .

<#q-release-r02-d2>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Only its license, since a beta is fine once tested"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Ready-to-use versions MUST only depend on ready-to-use versions, tested or not."@en .

<#q-release-r02-d3>
    a solid-memo:Distractor ,
      schema:Answer ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    solid-memo:distractorText "Neither, if the dependency is listed in the documentation"@en ;
    solid-memo:distractorNote "Documenting a dependency lifts neither MUST: the beta status and the license still rule it out."@en .
